/* ---------- O. PHAR6302 Tut 4 COPD breakout cases (14 Oct 2026) ---------- */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["copd", "<b>Brian, 59</b>: current smoker (35 pack-years), breathless 18 months, morning productive cough, no COPD diagnosis, no exacerbations. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.62, FEV&#8321; 72% predicted, CAAT 14.<br>Can the QLD Program take him with no prior diagnosis?", "<b>Yes &mdash; via targeted case-finding.</b> Aged &ge;35 with a risk factor (current smoker) &rarr; screening device &rarr; if the ratio suggests COPD, check eligibility, enrol, and conduct (or refer for) spirometry. A screening device <b>does not replace spirometry</b> for diagnosis.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Brian, 59</b>: current smoker (35 pack-years), breathless 18 months, morning productive cough, no COPD diagnosis, no exacerbations. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.62, FEV&#8321; 72% predicted, CAAT 14.<br>Interpret his spirometry.", "Ratio <b>0.62 &lt; 0.70</b> &rarr; airflow obstruction (must be <b>post-bronchodilator</b>). FEV&#8321; <b>72% = GOLD 2</b> (50&ndash;79%). QLD eligibility: FEV&#8321; &ge;60% &rarr; <b>still eligible</b>. GOLD grades and QLD bands are different scales &mdash; don&rsquo;t mix them.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / COPD pre-tutorial video 1 (GOLD 2026) / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Brian, 59</b>: current smoker (35 pack-years), breathless 18 months, morning productive cough, no COPD diagnosis, no exacerbations. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.62, FEV&#8321; 72% predicted, CAAT 14.<br>GOLD ABE group?", "<b>Group B</b>: no moderate/severe exacerbation in 12 months, but symptom burden high (<b>CAAT &ge;10</b>; 14). Group A needs CAAT &lt;10 <i>and</i> mMRC 0&ndash;1.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / COPD pre-tutorial video 1 (GOLD 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Brian, 59</b>: current smoker (35 pack-years), breathless 18 months, morning productive cough, no COPD diagnosis, no exacerbations. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.62, FEV&#8321; 72% predicted, CAAT 14.<br>What extra assessment before recommending treatment?", "&bull; Confirm <b>post-bronchodilator</b> result; asthma vs COPD features<br>&bull; <b>mMRC</b> dyspnoea, exacerbation history<br>&bull; Ineligibility screen: alarm signs (haemoptysis, chest pain, weight loss, night sweats, fever, oedema), cardiac cause, other respiratory disease<br>&bull; SpO&#8322;, RR, BMI, immunisation status, comorbidities, inhaler/device skills<br>&bull; Readiness to quit<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Brian, 59</b>: current smoker (35 pack-years), breathless 18 months, morning productive cough, no COPD diagnosis, no exacerbations. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.62, FEV&#8321; 72% predicted, CAAT 14.<br>Key management priorities?", "1. <b>Smoking cessation</b> &mdash; the single most beneficial measure: quit date, Quitline, NRT &plusmn; varenicline/bupropion<br>2. CAAT &ge;10 &rarr; pharmacotherapy likely needed (CAAT &lt;10 may need non-pharm only). Video: symptomatic &rarr; start <b>LAMA or LABA monotherapy</b><br>3. Pulmonary rehab, activity, written action plan, vaccines<br>4. Correct his belief that it&rsquo;s &lsquo;just ageing&rsquo;<br>5. <b>National Lung Cancer Screening Program</b>: aged 50&ndash;70, no symptoms, smokes or quit &le;10 years, &ge;30 pack-years &mdash; Brian (59, 35 pack-years) qualifies (Week 5)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Helen, 66</b>: on tiotropium, breathless on hills/carrying groceries, CAAT 19, no exacerbations in 12 months, adherent, good technique. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.59, FEV&#8321; 68%.<br>Assess her disease burden and treatment response.", "FEV&#8321; 68% = <b>GOLD 2</b>; no exacerbations + CAAT 19 = <b>GOLD B</b>. <b>Persistent symptoms on LAMA monotherapy</b> despite good adherence and technique = suboptimal response. Still QLD-eligible (FEV&#8321; &ge;60%, no exacerbations).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 2 / COPD pre-tutorial video 1 (GOLD 2026) / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Helen, 66</b>: on tiotropium, breathless on hills/carrying groceries, CAAT 19, no exacerbations in 12 months, adherent, good technique. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.59, FEV&#8321; 68%.<br>What do you check before changing treatment?", "<b>Inhaler technique and adherence</b> (re-check, don&rsquo;t assume), reliever use, other causes of breathlessness (cardiac, deconditioning, BMI), comorbidities, smoking/exposures, and that she is still eligible.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 2 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Helen, 66</b>: on tiotropium, breathless on hills/carrying groceries, CAAT 19, no exacerbations in 12 months, adherent, good technique. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.59, FEV&#8321; 68%.<br>Treatment option?", "Step up to <b>dual LAMA + LABA</b> (QLD Step 3) &mdash; persistent symptoms on monotherapy. Prefer a <b>single device</b>. <b>No ICS</b>: no exacerbations. Add <b>pulmonary rehabilitation</b> (consider in every symptomatic patient). Review <b>3 months</b> after a step-up.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 2 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>Helen, 66</b>: on tiotropium, breathless on hills/carrying groceries, CAAT 19, no exacerbations in 12 months, adherent, good technique. FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.59, FEV&#8321; 68%.<br>Which vaccines?", "<b>Influenza</b> annually &middot; <b>COVID-19</b> per national advice &middot; <b>pneumococcal</b> &middot; <b>RSV</b> (video: subsidised &ge;75 &rarr; self-funded at 66) &middot; <b>shingles</b> (video: subsidised &ge;65) &middot; <b>dTpa</b> (self-funded).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 2 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>John, 70</b>: on LAMA/LABA, <b>two exacerbations needing oral prednisolone</b> in 12 months, CAAT 24, FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.55, FEV&#8321; 54%, eosinophils 380 cells/&micro;L.<br>Most concerning features?", "&bull; <b>&ge;2 exacerbations needing systemic steroids</b> in 12 months<br>&bull; <b>FEV&#8321; 54% (&lt;60%)</b><br>&bull; Worsening on Step 3 (LAMA+LABA) so triple therapy would be needed<br>Each alone makes him <b>ineligible</b> for the QLD Program &rarr; refer. GOLD 2, <b>Group E</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 3 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 1 (GOLD 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>John, 70</b>: on LAMA/LABA, <b>two exacerbations needing oral prednisolone</b> in 12 months, CAAT 24, FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.55, FEV&#8321; 54%, eosinophils 380 cells/&micro;L.<br>What does eosinophils 380 cells/&micro;L add?", "ICS benefit runs on a gradient &mdash; <b>minimal around 100</b>, <b>greatest &gt;300</b> cells/&micro;L. With exacerbations despite LAMA/LABA, this supports escalation to <b>triple (ICS/LAMA/LABA)</b> &mdash; but that step is outside the pharmacist&rsquo;s protocol.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 3 / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "<b>John, 70</b>: on LAMA/LABA, <b>two exacerbations needing oral prednisolone</b> in 12 months, CAAT 24, FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.55, FEV&#8321; 54%, eosinophils 380 cells/&micro;L.<br>Next steps and what to hand over?", "<b>Refer to GP</b> with a comprehensive clinical handover: exacerbation history (2 &times; prednisolone), CAAT 24, spirometry, eosinophils, inhaler technique/adherence findings, current regimen. Options for the doctor/specialist: <b>triple single-inhaler</b> (lower mortality vs dual), pulmonary rehab, and for eosinophilic COPD still exacerbating on triple, <b>specialist biologics</b>. Pharmacist still: technique, action plan, vaccines, smoking.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 3 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd", "Brian, 59, current smoker, no COPD diagnosis. Post-bronchodilator FEV&#8321;/FVC 0.62, FEV&#8321; 72% predicted, no exacerbations, CAAT 14. GOLD classification?", ["GOLD 1, group A", "GOLD 2, group B", "GOLD 2, group E", "GOLD 3, group B"], 1, "FEV&#8321; 50&ndash;79% = GOLD 2. No exacerbations + CAAT &ge;10 = group B.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / COPD pre-tutorial video 1 (GOLD 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "Under the QLD COPD Program, a 59-year-old smoker with no previous COPD diagnosis presents with breathlessness. What is the correct pathway?", ["Ineligible &mdash; the Program needs an existing diagnosis", "Targeted case-finding with a screening device, then eligibility, enrolment and spirometry", "Diagnose COPD from the screening device result", "Start LAMA immediately and arrange spirometry later"], 1, "No prior diagnosis &rarr; targeted case-finding. A screening device does <b>not</b> replace spirometry for diagnosis.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 1 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "Helen, 66, on tiotropium, good technique and adherence, CAAT 19, no exacerbations, FEV&#8321; 68%. Best change?", ["Add an ICS to tiotropium", "Switch to a LABA alone", "Step up to LAMA + LABA, ideally in one device", "Refer &mdash; she is ineligible"], 2, "Persistent symptoms on monotherapy &rarr; dual bronchodilator (QLD Step 3). No exacerbations, so no ICS indication. Still eligible (FEV&#8321; &ge;60%).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 2 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026) / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "John, 70, on LAMA/LABA: two exacerbations needing oral prednisolone in 12 months, FEV&#8321; 54%, eosinophils 380. Under the QLD Program the pharmacist should:", ["Add an ICS to make triple therapy", "Prescribe a 5-day prednisolone course as a standing supply", "Refer &mdash; he meets several ineligibility criteria", "Continue and review in 6 months"], 2, "&ge;2 exacerbations needing systemic steroids, FEV&#8321; &lt;60%, and needing triple therapy after Step 3 &mdash; each makes him ineligible. Refer with a full handover.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 3 / QLD COPD Program protocol (Apr 2026). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "At what blood eosinophil count is the benefit of ICS in COPD greatest?", ["&gt;100 cells/&micro;L", "&gt;150 cells/&micro;L", "&gt;300 cells/&micro;L", "Eosinophils don&rsquo;t predict ICS response"], 2, "Gradient of benefit: minimal around 100, greatest &gt;300 cells/&micro;L.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"],
["copd", "Helen is 66 and has COPD. Per the pre-tutorial video, which vaccine is <b>self-funded</b> for her?", ["Influenza", "Shingles", "RSV", "None &mdash; all are funded"], 2, "Video: RSV subsidised from 75 (self-funded otherwise); shingles subsidised from 65; dTpa self-funded.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Tut 4 (Wk 8) COPD breakout case 2 / COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (Dr Zimmerman, GOLD 2026 / COPD-X). Tutorial gave questions only &mdash; model answer built from these sources.</div>"]
);


/* ---------- P. Smoking/vaping depth for Module Quiz 2 (15 Oct 2026) ----------
   Drafted from Week 5 pages + RACGP/QLD vaping guides; every quote machine-checked
   against the source (coursework/_review/drafts/smoke_quiz2.json). */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["smoke", "What is the Fagerstr&ouml;m Test for Nicotine Dependence (FTND), and how is it scored?", "A widely used questionnaire for the <b>intensity of physical nicotine dependence</b> in cigarette smokers.<br>&bull; <b>Six questions</b> on smoking behaviour (e.g. time to first cigarette after waking, cigarettes per day)<br>&bull; Score <b>0&ndash;10</b> &mdash; higher = greater dependence<br>&bull; Used to guide cessation interventions<br><i>The course page gives no score cut-off bands.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Patient History (Gather Information)</div>"],
["smoke", "What is the Heaviness of Smoking Index (HSI), and when would you use it?", "A <b>shortened FTND</b> using only <b>two items</b>:<br>&bull; Time to first cigarette after waking<br>&bull; Number of cigarettes smoked per day<br>Useful when a <b>rapid assessment</b> is needed; validated as a practical measure of nicotine dependence.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Patient History (Gather Information)</div>"],
["smoke", "Per the RACGP guideline, what is the most reliable single indicator of nicotine dependence, and what cut-off signals dependence?", "<b>Time to first cigarette.</b> Smoking <b>within 30 minutes of waking</b> indicates dependence.<br>Other indicators: &gt;10 cigarettes/day (some dependent people do not smoke daily) and withdrawal symptoms in previous quit attempts.<br>Cigarettes/day is now less robust &mdash; consumption has fallen and people under-report it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024) &mdash; Introduction to smoking cessation</div>"],
["smoke", "How do you assess nicotine dependence in someone who only vapes?", "Ask <b>Time to First Vape</b>: vaping, or a strong urge to vape, <b>within 30 minutes of waking</b> suggests high dependence. Also ask: vaped in the last 30 days? Difficulty stopping for hours or days (withdrawal)? Any cigarettes, shisha or cannabis (dual use)?<br>For young people, assess the <i>urge</i> &mdash; actual use may be delayed by the need to conceal it.<br>Course: harder than for smoking &mdash; patients may not know their nicotine concentration and vape continuously.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Qld Health E-cigarette and vaping cessation guide (Nov 2024); PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Selecting and Using Cessation Pharmacotherapies</div>"],
["smoke", "List the DSM-5 nicotine withdrawal symptoms named in the course.", "<b>Irritability, anxiety, difficulty concentrating, increased appetite, restlessness, depressed mood, insomnia</b> &mdash; causing clinically significant distress or impairment.<br>The history page also lists <b>cravings</b> and frustration/anger.<br>Withdrawal is a real, time-limited physiological syndrome &mdash; not a sign of weak willpower.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Understanding Nicotine Dependence and Vaping; Patient History (Gather Information)</div>"],
["smoke", "What is the typical time course of nicotine withdrawal?", "<b>Course:</b> peaks within the <b>first few days</b>, gradually resolves over <b>several weeks</b>; cravings can recur for longer, triggered by cues (stress, alcohol, seeing others smoke or vape).<br><b>RACGP:</b> usually begins within <b>24 h</b> of the last cigarette, strongest in the <b>first week</b> (for some only 2&ndash;3 days), and can disappear after about <b>2&ndash;4 weeks</b>. Can be reframed as &ldquo;recovery symptoms&rdquo;.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Understanding Nicotine Dependence and Vaping; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "Lapse vs relapse &mdash; define each, with the course&rsquo;s examples.", "<b>Lapse</b> (&ldquo;slip-up&rdquo;): a temporary return to smoking or vaping after abstinence &mdash; e.g. smoke-free 3 weeks, smokes two cigarettes at a party.<br><b>Relapse</b>: a return to regular smoking or vaping &mdash; the previous pattern &mdash; e.g. smoke-free 3 weeks, then resumes smoking daily.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "How should you respond to a lapse, and what should you avoid?", "Treat it as a <b>learning opportunity, not a failure</b>:<br>&bull; Reinforce success to date<br>&bull; Explore the trigger<br>&bull; Identify lessons learned<br>&bull; Encourage <b>immediate recommitment</b><br>&bull; Review pharmacotherapy &mdash; cravings controlled? adherence? dose adjustment? more support?<br><b>Avoid:</b> blaming, suggesting the attempt has failed, <b>stopping treatment prematurely</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "A patient has relapsed to regular smoking. Which treatment changes does the course list for the next attempt?", "&bull; <b>Combination NRT</b> instead of NRT monotherapy<br>&bull; <b>Varenicline</b> if not previously used<br>&bull; Improved adherence<br>&bull; Additional behavioural support<br>&bull; <b>Quitline referral</b><br>&bull; More frequent follow-up<br>First normalise it and reassess readiness; then build a revised quit plan, using the relapse as information.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "What are the steps of Ask&ndash;Advise&ndash;Help and of the 5As, and how does the course use each?", "<b>Ask&ndash;Advise&ndash;Help</b> (developed by Quit Victoria; RACGP-recommended <b>brief intervention</b>): <b>Ask</b> and record smoking status; <b>Advise</b> all who smoke to quit and on the most effective methods; <b>Help</b> &mdash; offer referral, behavioural support and evidence-based pharmacotherapy.<br><b>5As</b>: Ask, Assess, Advise, Assist, Arrange &mdash; the structure for a fuller consultation and comprehensive plan.<br>Behavioural support: <b>Quitline 13 78 48</b> or a tobacco treatment specialist.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Cessation Consultations for Pharmacist Prescribers; Patient History (Gather Information)</div>"],
["smoke", "What does Quitline provide?", "&bull; Confidential, evidence-based <b>telephone counselling</b> to stop smoking or vaping<br>&bull; <b>Multi-session</b> behavioural intervention<br>&bull; Tailored support for priority populations &mdash; pregnant people, young people, people with mental illness, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, people with substance use disorders<br>&bull; Interpreters available<br>Number: <b>13 78 48</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Developing a Smoking/Vaping Cessation Management Plan</div>"],
["smoke", "NRT: minimum duration and review schedule (Therapeutic Guidelines content in the course)?", "&bull; Adequate doses of <b>combination NRT for a minimum of 12 weeks</b><br>&bull; Review tolerability and effectiveness <b>2 weeks after starting</b>, then at intervals<br>&bull; Extended therapy may increase abstinence and reduce relapse<br>&bull; At completion, combination NRT can be <b>tapered or stopped abruptly</b> &mdash; patient preference<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "When do you review varenicline, and who should be offered a repeat course?", "Review <b>2 weeks</b> after starting and again at <b>12 weeks</b> (or earlier).<br>Offer a <b>repeat 12-week course to anyone who wishes to continue</b> &mdash; regardless of whether smoking has reduced &mdash; to increase the chance of prolonged abstinence.<br><i>PBS note (RACGP 2024): subsidy for the further 12 weeks requires abstinence at 12 weeks &mdash; a funding rule, not the clinical advice.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "Bupropion: when do patients usually stop smoking, when do you review, and what can be added?", "Usually stop smoking around the <b>second week</b> &mdash; but complete the full <b>9-week</b> course.<br>Review at <b>2 weeks</b> and again at <b>9 weeks</b> (or earlier).<br>Anecdotal evidence supports adding <b>medium- or fast-acting NRT after the first 2 weeks</b> for cue-driven cravings. Evidence is <b>lacking</b> for combining bupropion with varenicline.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "When should a pharmacist prescriber refer, or share care, in smoking/vaping cessation?", "<b>At follow-up, consider referral if:</b> significant mental health concerns; complex nicotine dependence; multiple unsuccessful attempts; coexisting substance use disorders; specialist behavioural support needed.<br><b>Referral or shared care:</b> pregnancy, breastfeeding, adolescents, significant mental illness, complex chronic disease, important medicine interactions, inadequate response, therapy outside scope.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review; Confirm, Communicate and Collaborate</div>"],
["smoke", "Since 1 October 2024, when can a pharmacist supply a nicotine vape without a prescription?", "&bull; Adult <b>18 or over</b><br>&bull; Nicotine <b>&le;20 mg/mL</b><br>&bull; Pharmacist satisfied it is clinically appropriate<br>&bull; Sights evidence of identity and age<br>&bull; <b>No more than one month&rsquo;s supply</b> at a time<br>&bull; Product on the TGA notified vape list (TGO 110); not ARTG-registered<br>A prescription (medical or nurse practitioner) is still needed for <b>&gt;20 mg/mL</b> or <b>under 18</b>. Subject to state/territory rules.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking &amp; vaping cessation (Oct 2024); PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Understanding Nicotine Dependence and Vaping</div>"],
["smoke", "RACGP Recommendation 15: if a nicotine vape is used for smoking cessation, what must the shared decision cover?", "Used only after failing first-line therapy (behavioural support + TGA-approved pharmacotherapy), <b>with behavioural support</b>. The patient should know:<br>&bull; Long-term health effects are unknown<br>&bull; NVPs are not registered therapeutic goods &mdash; safety and quality not established<br>&bull; Device and liquid variation adds risk<br>&bull; <b>Avoid dual use</b> and <b>minimise duration</b> of use<br>&bull; Return for regular review<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking &amp; vaping cessation (Oct 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "Smoking in pregnancy: what is first-line, and where does pharmacotherapy fit?", "<b>Course:</b> behavioural support is first-line; pharmacotherapy only after risk&ndash;benefit assessment with the medical practitioner; refer early.<br><b>RACGP:</b> if unable to quit with behavioural support, consider <b>NRT (usually oral forms)</b>, consulting the clinician supervising the pregnancy. <b>Varenicline, bupropion and nicotine vapes are not recommended</b> in pregnancy or breastfeeding. Nicotine clearance rises 60% in pregnancy, so NRT may be underdosed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Applying Clinical Reasoning to Cessation Management; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "Why is cessation a priority for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples? (course figures)", "&bull; Tobacco accounts for about <b>23% of the health gap</b><br>&bull; About <b>45% smoke daily</b> &mdash; almost 3&times; the non-Indigenous rate<br>&bull; Just as likely to <b>want to quit</b> and to have tried<br>&bull; Barriers: social norms, high stress, limited culturally appropriate services, language, lower uptake of cessation medicines<br>&bull; Vaping: about 8% (15+) currently vape, 22% have tried; most common at 18&ndash;24<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Smoking and Vaping Cessation and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People</div>"],
["smoke", "What clinical practice considerations does the course give for cessation support with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander patients?", "&bull; Culturally safe, respectful communication<br>&bull; <b>Ask permission</b> before discussing smoking or vaping<br>&bull; Recognise family, community and cultural connections<br>&bull; <b>Strengths-based</b>: explore motivations and strengths, not only risks<br>&bull; Offer evidence-based pharmacotherapy<br>&bull; Link to <b>local Aboriginal health services</b><br>&bull; Ongoing follow-up and relapse prevention<br>Cost: PBS-subsidised NRT and the <b>Closing the Gap PBS Co-payment Program</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Smoking and Vaping Cessation and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People</div>"],
["smoke", "What PBS NRT access do Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have (RACGP 2024)?", "&bull; Up to <b>two courses of nicotine patches a year</b>, each max <b>12 weeks</b><br>&bull; Support/counselling program <b>recommended, not mandatory</b><br>&bull; Nicotine gum and lozenge (2 mg and 4 mg) also listed<br>&bull; <b>No combination therapy</b> &mdash; one form at a time<br>&bull; Closing the Gap PBS co-payment reduces cost<br><i>Check current PBS listings &mdash; source dated 2024.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024) &mdash; Availability of smoking cessation medicines on the PBS</div>"],
["smoke", "Stopping smoking and CYP1A2: which drugs need monitoring, and what about caffeine?", "Aromatic hydrocarbons in smoke <b>induce CYP1A2</b>; stopping may <b>raise</b> levels. Monitoring/dose adjustment may be needed for <b>clozapine, olanzapine, warfarin, theophylline, erlotinib, pirfenidone</b> (wide-therapeutic-index drugs may not matter).<br><b>Reduce caffeine</b> &mdash; usual intake can cause anxiety, restlessness and insomnia mistaken for withdrawal.<br><b>Nicotine (NRT, vapes) does not induce CYP450.</b> Nicotine itself interacts with benzodiazepines and beta-blockers.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Understanding Nicotine Dependence and Vaping</div>"],
["smoke", "What does RACGP recommend about higher-dose NRT and pre-cessation patches?", "<b>Higher dose</b> &mdash; 4 mg gum/lozenge; 21 mg/24-h or 25 mg/16-h patch &mdash; for people with nicotine dependence, and for less dependent people still craving on the weaker form.<br><b>Preloading</b> (TGA-approved): 21 mg/24-h or 25 mg/16-h patch for <b>two weeks before quitting</b>, then continue, adding oral NRT if needed.<br>Dual patching: no clear superiority over one patch.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024) &mdash; Pharmacotherapy for smoking cessation</div>"],
["smoke", "Who is eligible for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program?", "Began <b>1 July 2025</b>; free Medicare-funded <b>low-dose CT</b>.<br>&bull; Aged <b>50&ndash;70</b><br>&bull; <b>No symptoms</b> of lung cancer<br>&bull; Currently smokes, or <b>quit within the past 10 years</b><br>&bull; Smoking history <b>&ge;30 pack-years</b><br>Referred by a healthcare provider; generally screened <b>every two years</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Physical Assessment and Smoking/Vaping Cessation</div>"],
["smoke", "Tutorial 3, Case 3 &mdash; Michael, 52: 25/day, first cigarette within 5 min of waking, relapsed at 3 weeks on patches alone, disliked gum; varenicline 3 years ago cut smoking substantially until a stressful event. Wants &ldquo;the option most likely to work&rdquo;. How does the course apply?", "<b>High dependence</b> (time to first cigarette, 25/day).<br>&bull; Varenicline = most effective monotherapy; it helped before &mdash; the relapse was trigger-driven<br>&bull; Combination NRT beats patch monotherapy &mdash; use a non-gum fast-acting form (lozenge or spray)<br>&bull; Plus Quitline, a stress plan, early follow-up<br><i>Slides give questions only &mdash; this answer is built from the Week 5 pages.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Tutorial 3 breakout cases; Selecting and Using Cessation Pharmacotherapies; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "Tutorial 3, Case 2 &mdash; Sarah, 34, newly diagnosed asthma, partner smokes: &ldquo;I know I should quit, but I&rsquo;m not ready yet.&rdquo; She asks about cutting down. What is the goal today?", "Not necessarily a quit date. Explore barriers and competing priorities, build rapport, find her own motivations (link to her asthma), introduce support options, <b>keep the conversation open</b>. Success can be <b>greater readiness</b> and a stronger therapeutic relationship.<br>RACGP: NRT can support <b>reduce-to-quit</b> for those not willing to quit immediately.<br><i>Slides give questions only &mdash; answer built from the Week 5 pages.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Tutorial 3 breakout cases; Developing a Smoking/Vaping Cessation Management Plan; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["smoke", "You need a rapid dependence check on a busy patient who smokes. Which items make up the Heaviness of Smoking Index?", ["Cigarettes per day and total pack-years smoked", "Time to first cigarette and withdrawal symptoms in past quit attempts", "Time to first cigarette after waking and cigarettes per day", "All six FTND questions, scored quickly from 0 to 10"], 2, "The HSI is a shortened FTND using two items: <b>time to first cigarette after waking</b> and <b>cigarettes per day</b>. The full FTND has six questions scored 0&ndash;10. Pack-years and past withdrawal are useful history but are not HSI items.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Patient History (Gather Information)</div>"],
["smoke", "Smoke-free for 3 weeks on combination NRT, a patient smoked two cigarettes at a party and says &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve blown it.&rdquo; What is this, and how should you respond?", ["A relapse &mdash; stop NRT and reassess readiness before restarting", "A lapse &mdash; reinforce progress, explore the trigger, continue treatment", "A relapse &mdash; the attempt has failed, so set a new quit date next month", "A lapse &mdash; pause NRT for a week, as smoking on NRT is dangerous"], 1, "A <b>lapse</b> is a temporary return after abstinence (the course&rsquo;s own example); a relapse is a return to regular use. Respond by reinforcing success, exploring the trigger and recommitting. Avoid suggesting the attempt has failed or stopping treatment prematurely. The Qld guide lists &ldquo;smoking while using NRT is dangerous&rdquo; as a myth: if a lapse occurs, don&rsquo;t stop NRT.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review; Qld Health E-cigarette and vaping cessation guide (Nov 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "One week after starting combination NRT, a patient reports nausea, sweating, dizziness and palpitations. What is the most appropriate next step?", ["Stop all NRT today and switch to varenicline", "Reassure that these are expected withdrawal symptoms and continue unchanged", "Halve the patch strength without asking anything more", "Assess further for nicotine excess, ongoing smoking or incorrect use"], 3, "The course lists possible explanations: <b>nicotine excess, continued smoking while using NRT, incorrect use, concurrent illness, anxiety</b>. &ldquo;The most appropriate response is not necessarily to stop treatment immediately. Further assessment is required.&rdquo; Changing the dose without assessment, or dismissing it as withdrawal, skips that step.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Selecting and Using Cessation Pharmacotherapies</div>"],
["smoke", "At the 12-week varenicline review, a patient has cut down but not stopped smoking and wants to keep going. What does the course&rsquo;s Therapeutic Guidelines content advise?", ["Offer a repeat 12-week course, as they wish to continue", "Stop varenicline &mdash; repeat courses are only for those who have quit", "Switch to bupropion, since varenicline has failed at 12 weeks", "Continue varenicline indefinitely with no further review point"], 0, "&ldquo;Offer a repeat 12-week course to anyone who wishes to continue (<b>regardless of whether their smoking has reduced</b>) to increase the likelihood of prolonged abstinence.&rdquo; Separate funding point: PBS subsidy for the extra 12 weeks requires abstinence at 12 weeks (RACGP 2024).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "In week 3 of bupropion, a patient stopped smoking in week 2 but has strong cue-driven cravings at work breaks. Which option is supported?", ["Add varenicline, as this combination is well supported", "Stop bupropion now, since the patient has already quit", "Consider adding a medium- or fast-acting NRT", "Extend bupropion to 24 weeks and review at the end"], 2, "Anecdotal evidence supports adding <b>medium- or fast-acting NRT</b> to bupropion after the first 2 weeks for cue-driven cravings. Evidence is <b>lacking</b> for bupropion + varenicline. Patients usually stop around week 2, but the <b>full 9-week course</b> should be completed, with review at 2 and 9 weeks.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["smoke", "A 26-year-old asks you to supply a 36 mg/mL nicotine vape without a prescription because 20 mg/mL &ldquo;isn&rsquo;t strong enough&rdquo;. What applies?", ["Supply is allowed if the vape is on the TGA notified list", "A prescription from a medical or nurse practitioner is required", "Pharmacists may supply any nicotine strength to adults for up to 3 months", "Supply is allowed once identity and age have been sighted"], 1, "Since 1 October 2024, people 18 or over can buy nicotine vapes of <b>20 mg/mL or less</b> from a pharmacist without a prescription, subject to conditions (clinically appropriate, ID and age sighted, <b>max one month&rsquo;s supply</b>). A prescription from a medical or nurse practitioner is still required for <b>&gt;20 mg/mL</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking &amp; vaping cessation (Oct 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "A woman 22 weeks pregnant cannot quit with behavioural support and Quitline and remains motivated. Which approach matches the sources?", ["Start varenicline, as the most effective monotherapy currently available", "Recommend a therapeutic nicotine vape at 20 mg/mL or less", "Start bupropion, as it is nicotine-free and safer for the fetus", "Consider NRT (usually oral) with the pregnancy clinician involved"], 3, "Course: behavioural support is first-line; pharmacotherapy only after risk&ndash;benefit assessment with the medical practitioner, and refer early. RACGP: if behavioural support fails, consider <b>NRT, usually oral forms</b>, and consult the clinician supervising the pregnancy. <b>Varenicline, bupropion and NVPs are not recommended</b> in pregnancy.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Applying Clinical Reasoning to Cessation Management; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"],
["smoke", "Aunty Mary, an Aboriginal woman who smokes 15 a day, wants NRT and is worried about cost. Which statement about PBS access (RACGP 2024) is correct?", ["Up to two 12-week patch courses a year; counselling not mandatory", "The PBS listing subsidises combination patch-plus-lozenge therapy", "A counselling program is mandatory for each subsidised course", "Only patches are listed; gum and lozenges must be bought over the counter"], 0, "Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples can get <b>up to two 12-week courses of patches a year</b>; a support program is <b>recommended but not mandatory</b>. Gum and lozenge (2 mg, 4 mg) are also listed, but <b>not two forms at once</b> &mdash; no combination subsidy. Closing the Gap PBS co-payment reduces cost. Check current PBS listings.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024); PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Smoking and Vaping Cessation and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People</div>"],
["smoke", "A 62-year-old who has smoked a pack a day for 35 years and still smokes has no respiratory symptoms. Is he eligible for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program?", ["No &mdash; the program is limited to people aged 50 to 60 years", "No &mdash; a history of at least 40 pack-years is required", "Yes &mdash; he meets the age, symptom and pack-year criteria", "No &mdash; only former smokers who quit within 10 years qualify"], 2, "Eligibility: aged <b>50&ndash;70</b>, <b>no symptoms</b>, <b>currently smokes or quit within the past 10 years</b>, and <b>&ge;30 pack-years</b>. One pack/day for 35 years = 35 pack-years. Free low-dose CT, generally every two years, from 1 July 2025.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Physical Assessment and Smoking/Vaping Cessation</div>"],
["smoke", "Two weeks after quitting smoking with NRT, a patient still drinking six strong coffees a day reports anxiety, restlessness and insomnia. Which explanation should you consider?", ["NRT nicotine is inducing CYP1A2, so caffeine levels have fallen", "Higher caffeine levels after loss of CYP1A2 induction by smoke", "Withdrawal alone &mdash; caffeine is unaffected by stopping smoking", "Nicotine withdrawal cannot cause insomnia, so it must be the patch"], 1, "Aromatic hydrocarbons in smoke induce CYP1A2, which metabolises caffeine. After quitting, keeping pre-cessation caffeine intake &ldquo;may result in anxiety, restlessness and insomnia which may be mistaken for nicotine withdrawal&rdquo;. <b>Nicotine (NRT, vapes) does not induce CYP450</b>. Insomnia is a recognised withdrawal symptom.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Understanding Nicotine Dependence and Vaping</div>"],
["smoke", "Tutorial 3, Case 2: Sarah, 34, newly diagnosed asthma, partner smokes, says &ldquo;I know I should quit, but I&rsquo;m not ready yet.&rdquo; What would success look like today?", ["Agreeing a firm quit date within the next two weeks", "Starting varenicline so she can quit when ready", "Deferring all smoking discussion until she raises it herself next time", "Greater readiness and rapport, with the conversation kept open"], 3, "For a patient not ready to quit, &ldquo;a successful consultation does not always result in a quit date&rdquo;. Explore barriers, build rapport and motivation, and keep the conversation open. Every conversation counts &mdash; deferring the topic misses the opportunity. Slides give questions only; this answer is built from the Week 5 pages.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Tutorial 3 breakout cases; Developing a Smoking/Vaping Cessation Management Plan</div>"],
["smoke", "Day 5 after quitting, a patient on combination NRT reports irritability, poor concentration and increased appetite and asks whether treatment is failing. Best response?", ["Yes &mdash; withdrawal on NRT means the dose is wrong; stop and switch", "These are patch side effects, so remove the patch at night", "Common withdrawal; usually strongest early and easing over weeks", "These suggest a new mood disorder needing urgent GP referral"], 2, "All three are DSM-5 withdrawal symptoms. &ldquo;Experiencing withdrawal does not mean treatment is failing.&rdquo; RACGP: strongest in the <b>first week</b>, can disappear after about <b>2&ndash;4 weeks</b>. Manage with continued pharmacotherapy, behavioural support, physical activity, healthy snacks and follow-up; check adherence and dose.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Putting It All Together; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (updated May 2024)</div>"]
);


/* ---------- Q. Quiz 2 practice set (Weeks 5-8), 19 Oct 2026 ----------
   20 practice MCQs Uzair supplied, with explanations and sources. Answers
   checked against eTG (asthma maintenance, stable COPD, NRT), QLD protocols,
   Week 5/8 pages and COPD videos. Q4 and Q6 rest on standard teaching. */
var _Q_FROM = COURSE_MCQ.length;
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["smoke", "A 23-year-old woman weighing 43 kg presents for review 5 weeks after commencing nicotine replacement therapy (NRT) for smoking cessation. She has remained abstinent from smoking but reports nausea, light-headedness and palpitations since her NRT regimen was intensified at her last review. She is otherwise well and wishes to continue treatment. Which of the following is the most appropriate management consideration?", ["Low body weight only affects dosing of fast-acting NRT products and is not relevant to nicotine patches.", "Her symptoms are most likely due to nicotine withdrawal and the NRT dose should be increased further.", "Her low body weight indicates that the initial dose of NRT was incorrectly selected and NRT should be ceased.", "Patients weighing less than 45 kg may be more susceptible to nicotine toxicity and may require lower maintenance doses of NRT."], 3, "eTG: &ldquo;starting doses of NRT are not dependent on weight, [but] patients who weigh less than 45 kg may be more likely to require lower maintenance doses of NRT to avoid toxicity.&rdquo; Nausea, dizziness and palpitations are the listed signs of <b>nicotine excess</b>, and they started when the dose went up &mdash; so reduce, don&rsquo;t stop or escalate. On combination NRT, reduce the fast/medium-acting form first.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Nicotine replacement therapy for tobacco smoking and nicotine dependence; RACGP Supporting smoking cessation</div>"],
["mh", "A patient reports restlessness, excessive worry, irritability and difficulty controlling thoughts about multiple aspects of their life. They deny persistent sadness or loss of interest. Which tool would provide the MOST targeted assessment?", ["WHO-5", "K10", "PHQ-9", "GAD-7"], 3, "Worry, restlessness and irritability without low mood = anxiety. <b>GAD-7</b> targets anxiety; PHQ-9 targets depression; K10 is general distress where the cause is unclear; WHO-5 is wellbeing. Tools never diagnose on their own.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &mdash; Mental Health Assessment in Primary Care</div>"],
["mh", "A 23-year-old patient seeks smoking cessation support. During the consultation they disclose cannabis use, unstable housing, relationship difficulties, withdrawal from study and periods of low mood. Which approach is MOST likely to provide useful information initially?", ["K10, because it provides a broad measure of psychological distress.", "GAD-7, because anxiety commonly affects smoking cessation attempts.", "PHQ-9, as depression may explain most of the presenting concerns.", "HEADSS assessment, as it explores multiple psychosocial domains contributing to health outcomes"], 3, "A young person with problems across many domains (home, education, drugs, relationships, mood) needs a <b>psychosocial</b> assessment first &mdash; <b>HEADSS</b>: Home, Education/employment, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide/mood. Single-symptom tools would miss most of the picture.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &mdash; Mental Health Assessment in Primary Care (HEADSS listed among the screening tools)</div>"],
["copd", "A 72-year-old patient with coexisting COPD, asthma, and heart failure is using frequent doses of a short-acting beta2 agonist (SABA). Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of this pattern of use?", ["It reflects optimal disease control and reduced cardiovascular risk.", "It improves cardiac outcomes by reducing myocardial oxygen demand.", "It has no clinically significant effect on heart rate or arrhythmia risk.", "It is a marker of poor disease control and is associated with increased risk of heart failure hospitalisation."], 3, "Frequent reliever use = <b>poor control</b> (reliever frequency is a control and risk marker in both QLD respiratory protocols). Beta2 agonists raise heart rate and arrhythmia risk, which matters in heart failure.<div class='alt'><span class='lbl'>Not in your files yet</span>Standard teaching &mdash; confirm against eTG Cardiovascular (Heart failure) when you have it.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD asthma/COPD protocols (reliever use as a control marker); cardiac link = standard teaching</div>"],
["mh", "Two patients both score 12 on a PHQ-9. Patient A remains employed, manages medications independently and remains socially engaged. Patient B has stopped attending appointments, withdrawn from family and is struggling to manage their diabetes. Which interpretation is MOST appropriate?", ["Patient B may require a more intensive intervention because the symptoms appear to be having greater impact on functioning.", "Both patients have comparable symptom severity and are therefore likely to require similar management.", "The difference in functioning is unlikely to be related to the symptoms identified through screening.", "The PHQ-9 score is more reliable than assessment of functioning when determining clinical significance."], 0, "Same score, different impact. Week 8 risk levels are driven by <b>function</b>: moderate = persistent symptoms <i>affecting function</i> &rarr; GP referral and plan. Tools never stand alone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &mdash; Mental Health Assessment in Primary Care &mdash; Recognising Risk and Knowing When to Escalate</div>"],
["copd", "A 65-year-old patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (LVEF 30%) and coexisting COPD is being reviewed for optimisation of therapy. The patient also has well-controlled asthma. Which of the following is the most appropriate statement regarding beta-blocker use in this patient?", ["Only non-selective beta-blockers should be used to maximise cardiovascular benefit.", "Cardio-selective beta-blockers such as metoprolol or bisoprolol should be avoided in severe airflow limitation.", "Cardio-selective beta-blockers can be safely used and should not be withheld, even with COPD and well-controlled asthma.", "Beta-blockers are contraindicated due to the presence of COPD and asthma."], 2, "Cardioselective beta-blockers (bisoprolol, metoprolol) should not be withheld because of COPD or well-controlled asthma. eTG <i>Overview of heart failure</i>: beta blockers &ldquo;improve heart function and reduce mortality and hospitalisation&rdquo;. Your course flags only <b>non-selective</b> beta-blockers as asthma triggers.<div class='alt'><span class='lbl'>Not in your files yet</span>Standard teaching &mdash; confirm against eTG Cardiovascular (Heart failure) when you have it.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 (non-selective beta-blockers as triggers); HFrEF benefit = standard teaching</div>"],
["asthma", "Anne is a 63-year-old woman with a confirmed diagnosis of asthma. She is currently prescribed low-dose fluticasone furoate-salmeterol twice daily plus salbutamol as needed for symptom control. She has experienced three asthma exacerbations in the past 12 months. What is the MOST APPROPRIATE treatment to recommend to reduce the risk of future exacerbations?", ["Step up to medium-dose fluticasone propionate-salmeterol twice daily plus salbutamol as needed", "Change to low-dose budesonide-formoterol maintenance-and-reliever therapy", "Change to medium-dose fluticasone furoate-vilanterol once daily plus salbutamol as needed", "Change to low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed"], 1, "eTG: &ldquo;ICS+formoterol maintenance and reliever therapy is associated with a <b>lower risk of exacerbations</b> than ICS+LABA maintenance plus SABA reliever therapy.&rdquo; Switching to MART beats increasing the ICS dose. As-needed only (option D) would be a step <i>down</i>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Step 3)</div>"],
["smoke", "A 38-year-old man presents seeking assistance to quit smoking. He reports smoking 10 cigarettes, but only on Friday evenings when socialising with a specific group of friends after work. He does not smoke at any other time and does not experience cravings outside these situations. Which of the following is the most appropriate statement regarding nicotine replacement therapy (NRT)?", ["A nicotine patch should be recommended because almost everyone who smokes is likely to benefit from slow-acting NRT.", "A nicotine patch may not be required because his smoking occurs only in highly specific cue-driven situations.", "Slow-acting NRT should be avoided because cue-driven smoking is best managed with bupropion.", "A nicotine patch is unlikely to provide benefit because he smokes fewer than 20 cigarettes per week."], 1, "Almost verbatim from eTG: &ldquo;those who only smoke in highly specific cue-driven situations <b>may not require a patch</b> (eg those who smoke after work with specific friends).&rdquo; The patch covers background craving, which he doesn&rsquo;t have &mdash; a fast-acting form for the situation fits.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Nicotine replacement therapy for tobacco smoking and nicotine dependence</div>"],
["copd", "A 62-year-old woman presents with gradually progressive exertional dyspnoea and a chronic productive cough. She has never smoked. A colleague states that chronic obstructive airway disease is unlikely because of the absence of a smoking history. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", ["Spirometry is not indicated unless there is a history of tobacco use.", "Chronic airflow limitation can occur in people who have never smoked and should still be considered.", "The absence of smoking history makes asthma the only likely diagnosis.", "Chronic airflow limitation is unlikely because smoking is required for the condition to develop."], 1, "COPD pathogenesis &ldquo;extends far beyond smoking&rdquo;: early-life factors (prematurity, childhood infections, asthma), air pollution, occupational exposures and socioeconomic factors. Symptoms + exposure history &rarr; consider COPD and confirm with post-bronchodilator spirometry.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 1 (Dr Zimmerman); QLD COPD protocol Appendix 1 (risk factors)</div>"],
["mh", "A pharmacist prescriber is reviewing a 74-year-old patient with heart failure. During medication review, the patient reports feeling &ldquo;down most days&rdquo; for several months and no longer enjoys spending time with family. The pharmacist has concerns about depression and wants a validated tool that can both assess symptoms and be repeated at follow-up consultations.", ["HEADSS", "K10", "PHQ-9", "MSE"], 2, "Low mood + loss of interest = depression. <b>PHQ-9</b> is the validated depression tool and can be repeated to track change. MSE is a structured clinical observation, not a repeatable score; K10 is general distress; HEADSS is for young people.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &mdash; Mental Health Assessment in Primary Care</div>"],
["copd", "An 80-year-old man with COPD is currently prescribed fluticasone furoate/umeclidinium/vilanterol 100/62.5/25 mcg once daily. He has a CAT score of 9, no exacerbations in the last 12 months and blood eosinophils are 100 cells/µL. What is the most appropriate recommendation?", ["Change to ICS/LABA", "Increase the dose of ICS/LAMA/LABA", "Change to LAMA/LABA", "No change"], 2, "No exacerbations and eosinophils ~100 &mdash; where ICS benefit is &ldquo;minimal&rdquo; (video 2). eTG: ICS increase pneumonia risk, and benefits &ldquo;must be balanced against the increased risk of pneumonia&rdquo;; review for step-down at each visit. So remove the ICS &rarr; LAMA/LABA.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2; eTG &mdash; Stable COPD</div>"],
["smoke", "A 35-year-old woman presents 1 week after starting nicotine gum for smoking cessation. She reports that the gum &ldquo;isn&rsquo;t working&rdquo; because she still experiences cravings. On further questioning, she says she chews the gum continuously like regular chewing gum and frequently swallows saliva while using it. Which advice is most likely to optimise nicotine delivery from the gum?", ["Use the &ldquo;chew and park&rdquo; technique and avoid swallowing saliva while chewing.", "Chew continuously for 30 minutes without pausing to maximise nicotine release.", "Swallow the saliva produced while chewing to improve nicotine absorption.", "Chew the gum harder and more frequently to release more nicotine."], 0, "eTG: chew until tingling, then <b>park</b> in the cheek; repeat for up to 30 minutes. &ldquo;Try not to swallow saliva&hellip; (swallowing saliva or the gum inactivates the nicotine). Chewing more often or harder doesn&rsquo;t release more nicotine.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Nicotine replacement therapy for tobacco smoking and nicotine dependence (Table 22.7)</div>"],
["asthma", "A 19-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with asthma. She experiences symptoms approximately once per week and has had no recent exacerbations. She is keen to use the least intensive treatment regimen that is still appropriate for her asthma. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment option?", ["Daily medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid with as-needed SABA", "Daily inhaled corticosteroid&ndash;LABA with as-needed SABA", "As-needed low-dose budesonide&ndash;formoterol", "Maintenance low-dose ICS&ndash;formoterol with as-needed ICS&ndash;formoterol (MART)"], 2, "Weekly symptoms is more than Step 1 (&lt;2/month) &rarr; <b>Step 2</b>. eTG: most people with asthma &ldquo;can be well controlled by Step 2 therapy; overtreatment with ICS+LABA&hellip; is common.&rdquo; Step 2 as-required therapy = low-dose budesonide+formoterol (the only approved product). Options A, B and D are more intensive.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Steps 1&ndash;2)</div>"],
["copd", "An 82-year-old man with a 50-pack-year smoking history undergoes post-bronchodilator spirometry because of mild exertional dyspnoea. His FEV₁/FVC ratio is 0.69, his FEV₁ is 83% predicted, the LLN for his FEV₁/FVC ratio is 0.65, and the corresponding z-score is −1.2. A colleague states that these results confirm persistent airflow limitation because the FEV₁/FVC ratio is below 0.7. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", ["The diagnosis is unlikely because the fixed ratio primarily under-diagnoses airflow limitation in older adults.", "The diagnosis is confirmed because an FEV₁/FVC ratio below 0.7 is diagnostic regardless of age.", "The diagnosis should be interpreted cautiously because the fixed FEV₁/FVC ratio may over-diagnose airflow limitation in older adults.", "Age does not affect interpretation of the FEV₁/FVC ratio."], 2, "QLD COPD protocol: the fixed ratio &ldquo;may lead to <b>over</b>-diagnosis in older populations, under-diagnosis in younger.&rdquo; His ratio 0.69 is <b>above</b> his LLN (0.65) and z-score &minus;1.2 is above the cut-off (video 1: below &minus;1.6) &mdash; likely normal ageing. Trap: option A says <i>under</i>-diagnoses.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD COPD protocol (spirometry NB); PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 1</div>"],
["asthma", "A 28-year-old man with asthma presents for review. He asks whether purchasing dust mite covers, removing carpets, and installing air filtration systems will improve his asthma control. Allergy testing has not identified any sensitivities, and he has not noticed any relationship between his symptoms and specific allergen exposures. What is the most appropriate advice?", ["Allergen avoidance strategies should only be considered if there is proven allergen sensitivity and the allergen is a clinically significant trigger of asthma symptoms.", "Avoidance of common household allergens is preferred to pharmacological therapy for long-term asthma management.", "Environmental allergen reduction measures should be routinely recommended for all patients with asthma.", "Dust mite reduction measures should be recommended because they improve asthma control regardless of allergy status."], 0, "QLD asthma protocol, verbatim: &ldquo;Do not routinely advise allergen avoidance or reduction measures&hellip; Allergen avoidance should only be considered if the patient has a proven sensitivity to the allergen, and the allergen is a clinically significant asthma trigger.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD asthma protocol (Apr 2026), citing the Australian Asthma Handbook &mdash; Asthma triggers</div>"],
["asthma", "A 19-year-old woman presents with recurrent wheeze, cough and chest tightness. Baseline spirometry does not demonstrate significant bronchodilator responsiveness. She is not currently using an ICS. To further investigate asthma, she is prescribed maintenance budesonide for 4 weeks. Before starting treatment, her pre-bronchodilator FEV₁ is 2.50 L. Four weeks later, her pre-bronchodilator FEV₁ is 2.85 L. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?", ["The response is clinically significant because the FEV₁ increased by more than 200 mL, regardless of the percentage change.", "The response is not clinically significant because ICS response should be assessed using post-bronchodilator spirometry.", "The response is clinically significant because the FEV₁ increased by 350 mL and 14% after 4 weeks of ICS treatment.", "The response is not clinically significant because an increase of at least 400 mL is required."], 2, "QLD asthma protocol: in those not already on ICS, a clinically significant increase in <b>pre</b>-bronchodilator FEV₁ of <b>&ge;200 mL and &ge;12%</b> after 4 weeks of maintenance ICS. 2.85 &minus; 2.50 = 350 mL; 350 &divide; 2500 = 14%. Both limbs are needed &mdash; option A drops the percentage.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD asthma protocol &mdash; spirometry findings consistent with variable expiratory limitation</div>"],
["smoke", "A 47-year-old man presents for review 2 weeks after commencing bupropion for smoking cessation. He reports that he stopped smoking completely 3 days ago. However, he has developed dry mouth, which he finds bothersome, and asks whether he can stop taking bupropion now that he has quit smoking. What is the most appropriate advice?", ["Continue bupropion and complete the full 9-week treatment course if it remains tolerable.", "Switch immediately from bupropion to nicotine replacement therapy.", "Cease bupropion now because smoking cessation has already been achieved.", "Stop bupropion and restart it only if smoking resumes."], 0, "Week 5: patients &ldquo;usually stop smoking around the second week of therapy, but the <b>full 9-week course</b> should be completed.&rdquo; Review at 2 and 9 weeks. Dry mouth is a nuisance effect, not a reason to stop.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &mdash; Monitoring and Review</div>"],
["copd", "Mr BL is 50 years old and has COPD. His post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC ratio is 0.56 and his FEV₁ is 38%. He currently uses ipratropium 1&ndash;2 puffs four times a day prn and salmeterol/fluticasone 50/250 mcg 1 puff twice a day. What is the most appropriate next step for him?", ["Continue with current inhalers and prescribe prednisolone 40 mg daily for 5 days", "Add terbutaline 500 mcg", "Replace ipratropium with terbutaline and add tiotropium 18 mcg", "Add tiotropium 18 mcg"], 2, "eTG: ipratropium &ldquo;is <b>contraindicated in patients taking a LAMA</b>&rdquo; (also in the QLD COPD protocol). Adding tiotropium means the ipratropium reliever must be swapped for a beta2-agonist reliever. Option D alone would leave both antimuscarinics. (FEV₁ 38% is outside the QLD Program &mdash; this is a general management question.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Stable COPD; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["smoke", "A 41-year-old woman presents for review 3 weeks after commencing varenicline for smoking cessation. She has remained abstinent and reports that her baseline nicotine cravings are well controlled. However, she experiences strong urges to smoke when drinking coffee or socialising with friends who smoke. She is otherwise tolerating treatment well. What is the most appropriate next step?", ["Discontinue varenicline and commence a repeat quit attempt if relapse occurs.", "Switch from varenicline to combination nicotine replacement therapy (NRT).", "Increase the varenicline dose to improve control of cue-driven cravings.", "Add a medium- or fast-acting NRT product while continuing varenicline."], 3, "RACGP: &ldquo;Combining varenicline with NRT may improve quit rates&rdquo;; varenicline + NRT gives higher abstinence than varenicline alone. Varenicline is controlling the background craving &mdash; add a fast/medium-acting NRT for the cue-driven urges. Don&rsquo;t stop or escalate varenicline.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (2024); PHAR6302 Week 5</div>"],
["mh", "A patient completes a K10 and scores in a range suggesting high psychological distress. During discussion they explain their partner died unexpectedly three weeks ago. Which interpretation is MOST appropriate?", ["The patient clearly has clinical depression.", "The screening result is invalid.", "The K10 result should be interpreted within the broader clinical context", "The score confirms an anxiety disorder"], 2, "K10 measures distress, cause unclear &mdash; it never diagnoses. Three weeks after sudden bereavement, high distress may be grief. The result is valid but must be read in context; still check risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &mdash; Mental Health Assessment in Primary Care</div>"]
);
PAPERS.push({id:"p6302q2mock", sem:"s2", course:"PHAR6302", week:"Weeks 5-8", title:"Quiz 2 practice set", note:"20 practice questions with explanations and sources.", from:_Q_FROM, count:20});


/* ---------- R. eTG respiratory + NRT (19 Oct 2026) ----------
   From eTG pages Uzair supplied: COPD exacerbations, antibiotic management of
   COPD exacerbations, pulmonary function testing, asthma maintenance, stable
   COPD, NRT. PACKS asthma/copd updated with the same eTG detail. */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["copd", "What prednis(ol)one dose does eTG give for a COPD exacerbation, and does it need tapering?", "<b>Prednis(ol)one 30&ndash;50 mg orally once daily for 5 days</b> &mdash; for exacerbations that don&rsquo;t respond enough to inhaled bronchodilators. Short courses can be <b>stopped abruptly without tapering</b>.<br>The QLD COPD protocol lets you prescribe up to 5 days, no repeats, via the action plan &mdash; and points to eTG for the regimen.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["copd", "COPD exacerbation: what reliever regimen does eTG give, and when must the patient seek medical attention?", "&bull; <b>Salbutamol 100 microg, up to 8 actuations one at a time via pMDI + spacer</b>, repeated as required<br>&bull; or terbutaline 500 microg 1&ndash;2 inhalations (DPI)<br>&bull; ipratropium only if <b>not</b> on a LAMA (slower onset)<br>Seek medical attention if symptoms stay uncontrolled or doses are needed <b>more often than 3-hourly</b> &mdash; then follow the next steps of the action plan.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "Which features of a COPD exacerbation indicate hospital admission (eTG Figure 9.15)?", "Inadequate response to ambulatory care · can&rsquo;t walk between rooms · can&rsquo;t eat or sleep from dyspnoea · can&rsquo;t manage at home · high-risk comorbidity (e.g. pneumonia) · altered mental state (hypercapnia) · worsening hypoxaemia or cor pulmonale · new or worsening central cyanosis · new arrhythmia · new peripheral oedema · haemodynamic instability · acute respiratory failure.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations (adapted from COPD-X)</div>"],
["copd", "What SpO₂ target does eTG set for oxygen in a COPD exacerbation, and why not higher?", "Give oxygen if SpO₂ <b>&lt;88%</b>, titrated to <b>88&ndash;92%</b>. Patients with COPD risk <b>hypercapnic respiratory failure</b>, and excessive oxygen is associated with increased mortality. Nebulisers should be driven by <b>compressed air, not oxygen</b>.<br>Compare the QLD protocol safety-net: SpO₂ falling <i>towards 92%</i> &rarr; emergency care.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["copd", "What should happen after any COPD exacerbation (eTG post-acute care)?", "&bull; Review regular therapy, <b>adherence and inhaler technique</b><br>&bull; Update the written <b>COPD action plan</b> and check they can use it<br>&bull; Refer to <b>pulmonary rehabilitation</b> (fewer readmissions, better quality of life)<br>&bull; GP review within <b>7 days</b> of discharge<br>&bull; A hospital admission is a <b>sentinel event</b> &mdash; consider advance care planning<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "When does eTG recommend antibiotics for a COPD exacerbation?", "<b>Not routinely.</b><br>&bull; <b>Give</b>: severe exacerbation needing ICU or ventilation<br>&bull; <b>Consider</b>: increased <b>sputum purulence</b> with increased sputum volume and/or increased dyspnoea<br>&bull; <b>Withhold safely</b>: less severe exacerbations managed in the community<br>A low CRP supports withholding. Under the QLD Program a suspected bacterial infection = <b>refer</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Antibiotic &mdash; Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["copd", "Which antibiotics are first-line for a COPD exacerbation (if indicated), and which are not?", "<b>Amoxicillin 500 mg 8-hourly</b> or <b>1 g 12-hourly</b>, or <b>doxycycline 100 mg daily</b> &mdash; all <b>5 days</b>, oral. Doses are lower than for pneumonia because the aim is faster recovery, not eradicating colonisation.<br><b>Not first-line:</b> amoxicillin+clavulanate, macrolides, cephalosporins &mdash; no more effective, more harm.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Antibiotic &mdash; Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "Is sputum culture routine in a COPD exacerbation? When is it considered?", "<b>Not routine</b> &mdash; many patients are colonised (H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis, S. pneumoniae), so a positive culture doesn&rsquo;t prove infection. Consider if: FEV₁ &lt;50% · &ge;2 exacerbations a year · hospitalised in past 3 months · Pseudomonas in past 3 months · home oxygen.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Antibiotic &mdash; Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "What features suggest pneumonia rather than a simple COPD exacerbation?", "Tachypnoea at rest, tachycardia, rigors, and <b>crackles that don&rsquo;t clear with coughing</b> &mdash; chest X-ray if indicated. Under the QLD protocol, suspected pneumonia = emergency care / refer.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Antibiotic &mdash; Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["copd", "According to eTG, when is an inhaled corticosteroid added to LAMA+LABA in stable COPD?", "Only if <b>both</b>: a <b>severe exacerbation (hospitalised) or &ge;2 moderate exacerbations</b> in 12 months, <b>and</b> significant symptoms despite LAMA+LABA. Before stepping up, check <b>inhaler technique and adherence</b> and consider other causes of breathlessness. ICS increase pneumonia risk. Needing triple therapy puts the patient outside the QLD Program.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Stable COPD; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["copd", "Stable COPD: why is LAMA+LABA preferred over ICS+LABA as dual therapy (eTG)?", "LAMA+LABA improves lung function and quality of life versus monotherapy and <b>appears to reduce exacerbations compared with ICS+LABA</b> &mdash; without the pneumonia risk of an ICS. Review 3&ndash;6 months after any change.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Stable COPD</div>"],
["asthma", "eTG asthma Steps 1&ndash;3: what is indicated at each step?", "<b>Step 1</b> (symptoms &lt;2/month, no risk factors): as-required low-dose budesonide+formoterol (eTG also lists as-required SABA).<br><b>Step 2</b> (symptoms or reliever &ge;2/month): as-required low-dose budesonide+formoterol <i>or</i> regular low-dose ICS. Most patients are controlled here.<br><b>Step 3</b> (uncontrolled on optimised Step 2): low-dose ICS+LABA in one inhaler, or ICS+formoterol <b>MART</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents</div>"],
["asthma", "<b>eTG vs QLD protocol:</b> eTG Step 1 lists as-required SABA. What does the QLD asthma protocol say?", "QLD (citing the Australian Asthma Handbook): <b>&ldquo;As-needed SABA alone (without ICS) should never be prescribed or recommended&rdquo;</b> &mdash; all adults and adolescents should be prescribed ICS, even if symptoms are infrequent. The protocol says follow the AAH where it differs from eTG &mdash; so under the Program, never SABA alone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD asthma protocol (Apr 2026); eTG Respiratory &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents</div>"],
["asthma", "Why does eTG prefer ICS+formoterol MART over ICS+LABA + SABA for a patient having exacerbations?", "MART is &ldquo;associated with a <b>lower risk of exacerbations</b> than ICS+LABA maintenance plus SABA reliever therapy.&rdquo; Budesonide+formoterol (or beclometasone+formoterol at low dose only) can be used as MART. Always give ICS+LABA as one inhaler &mdash; LABA alone increases exacerbations and asthma death.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents</div>"],
["asthma", "How does eTG step down asthma treatment?", "&bull; Medium/high-dose ICS: cut the dose <b>25&ndash;50% every 2&ndash;3 months</b><br>&bull; Low-dose ICS+LABA &rarr; ICS alone<br>&bull; Regular budesonide+formoterol &rarr; <b>as-required</b> budesonide+formoterol (better than SABA alone)<br>Stopping ICS completely carries a significant exacerbation risk (QLD: never withdraw ICS completely).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents; QLD asthma protocol</div>"],
["copd", "Name the relative contraindications to spirometry listed by eTG.", "MI in the previous week · syncope with forced expiration or cough · recent concussion with symptoms · eye, sinus or middle-ear surgery in the previous week · middle-ear infection in the previous week · current or recent <b>pneumothorax</b> · thoracic or abdominal surgery in the previous 4 weeks · current <b>haemoptysis</b>. Relative &mdash; weigh risk and benefit; list them on any referral.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Pulmonary function testing</div>"],
["copd", "Obstructive vs restrictive pattern on spirometry (eTG Table 9.27)?", "<b>Obstructive:</b> <b>low FEV₁/FVC ratio</b>, concave flow&ndash;volume loop &mdash; asthma, COPD, overlap.<br><b>Restrictive:</b> <b>low FVC</b>, normal or increased ratio, small-volume loop &mdash; interstitial lung disease, respiratory muscle weakness, chest wall disease, obesity. (Restrictive/mixed spirometry makes a patient ineligible for the QLD COPD Program.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Pulmonary function testing; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["asthma", "Can a peak flow meter diagnose asthma? Is it useful in COPD?", "<b>No</b> &mdash; it can&rsquo;t replace spirometry for diagnosis. But if spirometry shows no reversibility and suspicion stays high, <b>repeated</b> peak flows can show variable obstruction. Always use the <b>same meter</b>. <b>Not useful in COPD</b> &mdash; the obstruction isn&rsquo;t variable.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Pulmonary function testing</div>"],
["copd", "What are the limits of pulse oximetry (eTG)?", "Measures saturation only &mdash; <b>not CO₂</b> (needs arterial blood gas). Misleading with poor circulation, poor technique, faulty device, nail polish, <b>dark skin</b>, high <b>carboxyhaemoglobin</b> (carbon monoxide, heavy smoking &mdash; falsely high), methaemoglobin, cyanide or severe anaemia (falsely reassuring).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Pulmonary function testing</div>"],
["smoke", "NRT toxicity on combination therapy: which product do you reduce first, and when do you review (eTG)?", "Excess nicotine causes dizziness, nausea and palpitations. On combination NRT, <b>reduce the fast- or medium-acting form before</b> touching the patch. If still smoking, explain that it adds to the symptoms. Review <b>2 weeks</b> after starting and through a <b>minimum 12-week</b> course. Under 45 kg may need lower maintenance doses.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG &mdash; Nicotine replacement therapy for tobacco smoking and nicotine dependence</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd", "A patient with COPD on tiotropium has an exacerbation. Which initial reliever regimen matches eTG?", ["Ipratropium 21 microg up to 4 puffs via spacer, repeated as required", "Salbutamol 100 microg up to 8 puffs one at a time via spacer, repeated as required", "Salbutamol via oxygen-driven nebuliser at 8 L/min", "No reliever &mdash; start prednisolone immediately"], 1, "Salbutamol 100 microg up to 8 actuations via pMDI+spacer, repeated as required. Ipratropium is contraindicated with a LAMA (tiotropium). Nebulisers should be air-driven, not oxygen. Steroids are added if the bronchodilator response is insufficient.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "A COPD patient on their action plan asks how long to take prednisolone and whether to taper. Per eTG:", ["30&ndash;50 mg daily for 5 days, then stop without tapering", "40 mg daily for 14 days, then taper over a week", "10 mg daily until the cough settles", "50 mg daily for 5 days, then halve the dose for 5 days"], 0, "eTG: prednis(ol)one 30&ndash;50 mg once daily for 5 days; short courses can be stopped abruptly without tapering.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "Which COPD exacerbation is antibiotic therapy most clearly NOT needed for (eTG)?", ["Severe exacerbation needing non-invasive ventilation", "Increased sputum purulence and volume with increased dyspnoea", "A mild exacerbation managed in the community with no change in sputum", "Exacerbation in a patient needing ICU admission"], 2, "Antibiotics can be safely withheld for less severe community-managed exacerbations. They are given for ICU/ventilation cases and considered when sputum purulence rises with volume and/or dyspnoea.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Antibiotic &mdash; Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations</div>"],
["copd", "If antibiotics are indicated for a COPD exacerbation, which is a first-line choice (eTG)?", ["Amoxicillin+clavulanate 875+125 mg 12-hourly", "Clarithromycin 500 mg 12-hourly", "Doxycycline 100 mg daily for 5 days", "Cefuroxime 500 mg 12-hourly"], 2, "First-line: amoxicillin 500 mg 8-hourly or 1 g 12-hourly, or doxycycline 100 mg daily, for 5 days. Amoxicillin+clavulanate, macrolides and cephalosporins are not recommended initially. Under the QLD Program, refer &mdash; pharmacists don&rsquo;t prescribe them.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Antibiotic &mdash; Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["copd", "A patient with COPD on LAMA+LABA had one moderate exacerbation this year and has mild symptoms. Per eTG, should an ICS be added?", ["Yes &mdash; any exacerbation warrants triple therapy", "No &mdash; ICS needs a severe or &ge;2 moderate exacerbations AND significant symptoms on dual therapy", "Yes &mdash; ICS replaces the LAMA", "No &mdash; ICS are never used in COPD"], 1, "eTG: add ICS only if a severe (hospitalised) or &ge;2 moderate exacerbations in 12 months AND significant symptoms on dual therapy. Check technique and adherence first. ICS raise pneumonia risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Stable COPD</div>"],
["copd", "Spirometry shows a low FVC with a normal FEV₁/FVC ratio. What pattern is this?", ["Obstructive", "Restrictive", "Normal", "Reversible obstruction"], 1, "eTG Table 9.27: restrictive = low FVC with normal or increased FEV₁/FVC (e.g. ILD, muscle weakness, chest wall disease, obesity). Obstructive = low ratio. Restrictive spirometry makes the patient ineligible for the QLD COPD Program.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Pulmonary function testing; QLD COPD protocol</div>"],
["asthma", "Under the QLD asthma protocol, a new patient with symptoms once a month asks for a salbutamol-only reliever. What is correct?", ["Prescribe as-needed salbutamol alone &mdash; eTG Step 1 allows it", "As-needed SABA alone should never be prescribed; ICS-containing therapy for all", "Prescribe montelukast instead", "No treatment is needed below twice-monthly symptoms"], 1, "Although eTG Step 1 lists as-required SABA, the QLD protocol (following the Australian Asthma Handbook, which it says takes precedence) states SABA alone should never be prescribed; all adults and adolescents get ICS. Montelukast must not be initiated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD asthma protocol (Apr 2026); eTG Respiratory &mdash; Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents</div>"],
["copd", "A heavy smoker&rsquo;s SpO₂ reads 98%. Why might this be falsely reassuring?", ["Smoking lowers haemoglobin", "High carboxyhaemoglobin can give a falsely high reading", "Pulse oximeters over-read in everyone over 60", "Nicotine causes peripheral vasodilation"], 1, "eTG: pulse oximeters may read falsely high with high carboxyhaemoglobin (carbon monoxide, chronic heavy smoking). Oximetry also doesn&rsquo;t measure CO₂.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Pulmonary function testing</div>"]
);


/* ---------- S. Drug classes: respiratory, CV, lipids, smoking, vaccines + COPD-X (19 Oct 2026) ----------
   Agent-drafted from eTG, COPD-X, LFA, Immunisation Handbook, QLD protocols, RACGP;
   every evidence quote machine-checked (coursework/_review/drafts/drugs_*.json).
   DRUGS is ext-only, so pushing onto it is index-safe. */
DRUGS.push(
{"id": "saba", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Short-acting beta2 agonist (SABA)", "tag": "asthma · COPD", "agents": ["salbutamol (Ventolin, Asmol, Airomir)", "terbutaline (Bricanyl Turbuhaler)"], "moa": "Short-acting bronchodilator taken as required for short-term symptom relief. In COPD there is no evidence it slows lung-function decline or affects survival (eTG).", "ci": "", "cautions": "Asthma: frequent use or monotherapy is linked to poor outcomes. Using &ge;3 salbutamol canisters a year raises exacerbation risk; &gt;12 a year raises asthma-death risk. Frequent use causes beta-receptor downregulation, rebound hyperresponsiveness and a reduced bronchodilator response (eTG).", "se": "Repeated high doses: hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia (anticipate them). Cardiovascular effects, eg myocardial ischaemia and prolonged QT predisposing to arrhythmias (eTG COPD exacerbations).", "admin": "<b>Asthma:</b> salbutamol 200 mcg pMDI + spacer, or terbutaline 500 mcg DPI, prn (eTG Step 1). Before exercise: 1 dose 15 min beforehand (QLD).<br><b>Stable COPD:</b> salbutamol 100&ndash;200 mcg prn.<br><b>COPD flare:</b> salbutamol 100 mcg/actuation, up to 8 actuations one at a time via spacer, repeated as required. Needed more often than 3-hourly = seek medical attention.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>QLD/AAH:</b> never SABA alone in asthma, even with infrequent symptoms; every patient gets ICS. &ge;12 SABA canisters a year = treat and concurrently refer. <b>Conflict:</b> eTG Step 1 still lists SABA alone prn. The action plan must state the maximum single dose and maximum 24-hour dose. COPD: SABA or SAMA is the rescue reliever at every step.", "src": "QLD asthma + COPD protocols; eTG asthma maintenance, stable COPD, COPD exacerbations; COPD-X Handbook"},
{"id": "sama", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Short-acting muscarinic antagonist (SAMA) &mdash; ipratropium", "tag": "COPD", "agents": ["ipratropium (Atrovent pMDI 21 mcg)"], "moa": "Blocks only the muscarinic effects of acetylcholine, so COPD-X calls it antimuscarinic, not anticholinergic. It acts on the same muscarinic receptors as a LAMA, so the two overlap (Week 6).", "ci": "<b>Contraindicated with a LAMA</b>, including LAMA-containing combinations (QLD COPD protocol; eTG). Lung Foundation: DO NOT USE SAMA with any LAMA.", "cautions": "Slower onset than salbutamol. eTG: not usually used for symptom relief in COPD, because it costs more than a SABA and may increase cardiovascular events.", "se": "Increased cardiovascular risk. COPD-X: the consistent finding across studies (combined CV death, MI and stroke) suggests the effect is likely real.", "admin": "<b>Stable:</b> 21 mcg pMDI, 2 inhalations as required (eTG Table 9.15).<br><b>Exacerbation, only if not on a LAMA:</b> 21 mcg/actuation, up to 4 actuations one at a time via spacer, repeated as required. Nebuliser: 250&ndash;500 mcg, driven by air, not oxygen. Adding salbutamol may give extra benefit without compounding adverse effects.", "pbs": "", "prog": "A Program reliever option for COPD flares under eTG, but <b>check for a LAMA first</b>. Week 6: salbutamol is generally preferred for its rapid onset. eTG: salbutamol, terbutaline and ipratropium relieve exacerbation symptoms equally well. If a LAMA is started, remove ipratropium from the action plan.", "src": "QLD COPD protocol; eTG stable COPD + exacerbations; COPD-X 2.78; LFA Stepwise 2026; Week 6 Managing Exacerbations"},
{"id": "laba", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Long-acting beta2 agonist (LABA)", "tag": "COPD · asthma (only with ICS)", "agents": ["indacaterol (Onbrez Breezhaler)", "formoterol", "olodaterol", "salmeterol", "vilanterol"], "moa": "Long-acting bronchodilator. eTG (COPD): improves FEV1, breathlessness and exercise capacity; reduces exacerbations and hospitalisations; improves quality of life. Benefits appear within 6&ndash;12 weeks, even without a change in FEV1.", "ci": "<b>Asthma: never without ICS</b> (QLD). LABA monotherapy increases exacerbations and asthma-related death (eTG). Asthma + COPD overlap: never without ICS (QLD).", "cautions": "Cardiac safety is less clear with overdosing, or in COPD with substantial cardiovascular disease, prolonged QTc or polypharmacy (COPD-X).", "se": "Cardiovascular evidence conflicts (COPD-X). One meta-analysis found beta2 agonists raised CV event risk (sinus tachycardia RR 3.06). Another found fewer fatal CV events with LABAs.", "admin": "COPD monotherapy: indacaterol 150 mcg DPI daily, increased to 300 mcg daily if required (eTG). Other LABAs are only used in combination inhalers. Assess at 3&ndash;6 months; check technique and adherence first.", "pbs": "Indacaterol is the only LABA on the PBS as monotherapy for COPD (eTG, at time of writing).", "prog": "COPD Program Step 2 = LABA <i>or</i> LAMA monotherapy. eTG: LAMAs cut exacerbations more than LABAs in meta-analysis, but individual response varies. Week 6: eTG says LAMA or LABA at Step 2; NICE starts with LAMA + LABA. In asthma, a LABA only ever comes in an ICS combination inhaler.", "src": "QLD asthma + COPD protocols; eTG stable COPD, asthma maintenance; COPD-X 2.78; Week 6 Management Plan"},
{"id": "lama", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA)", "tag": "COPD · asthma add-on (Step 4)", "agents": ["aclidinium (Bretaris Genuair)", "glycopyrronium (Seebri Breezhaler)", "tiotropium (Spiriva Handihaler, Braltus Zonda, Spiriva Respimat)", "umeclidinium (Incruse Ellipta)"], "moa": "Blocks the muscarinic effects of acetylcholine, giving bronchodilation for 12&ndash;24 hours depending on the agent (COPD-X).", "ci": "Do not combine with ipratropium (SAMA): contraindicated (QLD, eTG, Lung Foundation). Asthma + COPD overlap: never without ICS (QLD).", "cautions": "", "se": "Dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (COPD-X).", "admin": "<b>COPD</b> (eTG):<br>&bull; aclidinium 322 mcg DPI bd<br>&bull; glycopyrronium 50 mcg DPI daily<br>&bull; tiotropium 13 or 18 mcg DPI daily (both deliver 10 mcg; interchangeable), or 2.5 mcg Respimat, 2 puffs daily<br>&bull; umeclidinium 62.5 mcg DPI daily<br><b>Asthma</b> (eTG Step 4 add-on): tiotropium 5 mcg mist inhaler daily.", "pbs": "All four LAMAs are on the Lung Foundation 2026 chart, which lists only inhalers PBS-subsidised for COPD.", "prog": "COPD Program Step 2 = LAMA <i>or</i> LABA. eTG: LAMAs reduced exacerbations more than LABAs in meta-analysis. Starting a LAMA means taking ipratropium off the action plan; salbutamol becomes the reliever. In asthma, a LAMA appears only at eTG Step 4. The Program refers patients who need AAH Level 4.", "src": "QLD asthma + COPD protocols; eTG stable COPD, asthma maintenance; COPD-X 2.78; LFA Stepwise 2026"},
{"id": "ics", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Inhaled corticosteroid (ICS)", "tag": "asthma (all patients) · COPD (selected)", "agents": ["beclometasone (Qvar)", "budesonide (Pulmicort)", "ciclesonide (Alvesco)", "fluticasone", "mometasone"], "moa": "Preventer. Asthma: waking at night can improve after a week; lung function can take months (eTG). In asthma + COPD overlap, ICS reduces the risk of serious exacerbations and death (QLD).", "ci": "", "cautions": "COPD + diabetes: do not exceed the recommended corticosteroid dose, because dose relates to glucose (COPD-X). Pneumonia risk rises with dose, so use the lowest dose. It is highest with fluticasone and was not seen with budesonide (COPD-X). Long-term high-dose ICS is a risk factor for treatment harm (eTG).", "se": "Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia/hoarseness. In COPD, pneumonia: risk up 41% with &ge;1 year of ICS. Effects on diabetes, bones and eyes are less clear (COPD-X). A spacer with a pMDI makes oral thrush less likely (COPD video 2).", "admin": "Asthma low dose (eTG Step 2, plus SABA prn):<br>&bull; beclometasone 50 or 100 mcg bd<br>&bull; budesonide 100 or 200 mcg bd<br>&bull; ciclesonide 80 or 160 mcg daily<br>&bull; fluticasone propionate 50 or 100 mcg bd<br>Flare on ICS alone: quadruple the dose for 1&ndash;2 weeks (doubling is not enough).", "pbs": "Single-drug ICS inhalers are not PBS-approved for COPD, so in COPD ICS comes as ICS+LABA or triple therapy (eTG).", "prog": "<b>Asthma:</b> every adult and adolescent must be prescribed ICS; never withdraw it completely (QLD, Apr 2026). Step down one Level after 2&ndash;3 months of good control with low exacerbation risk; review in 4&ndash;8 weeks. <b>COPD:</b> ICS only as triple therapy. Starting triple is outside the Program: refer. Asthma + COPD overlap: asthma protocol, with ICS.", "src": "QLD asthma + COPD protocols; summary of changes Apr 2026; eTG asthma maintenance, stable COPD; COPD-X 2.78; COPD video 2"},
{"id": "icslaba", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "ICS + LABA combination (incl. ICS-formoterol AIR and MART)", "tag": "asthma · COPD (as part of triple)", "agents": ["budesonide+formoterol (Symbicort, DuoResp)", "beclometasone+formoterol", "fluticasone propionate+formoterol", "fluticasone propionate+salmeterol (Seretide)", "fluticasone furoate+vilanterol (Breo)", "mometasone+indacaterol"], "moa": "ICS preventer plus long-acting bronchodilator in one device. Formoterol is a rapid-onset beta2 agonist, so low-dose ICS-formoterol can also be the reliever (eTG).", "ci": "In asthma, never give the LABA as a separate inhaler; always use a combination inhaler (eTG). Beclometasone+formoterol is not TGA-approved under 18 years.", "cautions": "Budesonide+formoterol 400+12 is not for reliever use. The 200+6 pMDI is not TGA-approved for MART; use the 50+3 or 100+3 pMDI, or the 100+6 or 200+6 DPI. COPD: fluticasone furoate+vilanterol 200+25 increases adverse effects, including pneumonia, with no outcome benefit (eTG).", "se": "", "admin": "<b>AIR</b> (eTG Steps 1&ndash;2): budesonide+formoterol 200+6 mcg prn.<br><b>MART</b> (eTG Step 3):<br>&bull; budesonide+formoterol 100+6 or 200+6 bd + prn, max 2400+72 mcg/day<br>&bull; beclometasone+formoterol 100+6 bd + prn, max 800+48 mcg/day<br><b>COPD triple</b> (+ separate LAMA): budesonide+formoterol 400+12 bd, FF/VI 100+25 daily, or FP/salmeterol 500+50 bd.", "pbs": "Beclometasone+formoterol is not PBS-listed as MART (eTG, at time of writing).", "prog": "<b>QLD/AAH:</b> most patients can start and stay on as-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol. AIR needed &ge;3 times a week: switch to MART. Move from the alternative (SABA reliever) to the recommended option before raising the dose. Refer if uncontrolled on recommended Level 3. eTG: MART has fewer exacerbations than ICS+LABA plus SABA. In COPD, LAMA+LABA beats ICS+LABA on exacerbations.", "src": "QLD asthma protocol; eTG asthma maintenance, stable COPD"},
{"id": "lamalaba", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "LAMA + LABA dual bronchodilator", "tag": "COPD (Program Step 3)", "agents": ["formoterol+aclidinium (Brimica Genuair)", "indacaterol+glycopyrronium (Ultibro Breezhaler)", "olodaterol+tiotropium (Spiolto Respimat)", "vilanterol+umeclidinium (Anoro Ellipta)"], "moa": "Two long-acting bronchodilators. Versus monotherapy: better lung function and quality of life. Fewer exacerbations than LABA alone, possibly than LAMA alone, and apparently than ICS+LABA (eTG).", "ci": "No ipratropium (SAMA), because the inhaler contains a LAMA. Asthma + COPD overlap: never without ICS (QLD).", "cautions": "Before stepping up, check technique and adherence and consider other causes of breathlessness (eTG).", "se": "As for LAMA (dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention) and LABA (COPD-X).", "admin": "Fixed-dose combinations (eTG):<br>&bull; formoterol+aclidinium 12+340 mcg bd<br>&bull; indacaterol+glycopyrronium 110+50 mcg daily<br>&bull; olodaterol+tiotropium 2.5+2.5 mcg Respimat, 2 puffs daily<br>&bull; vilanterol+umeclidinium 25+62.5 mcg daily<br>Or two single inhalers at monotherapy doses. Assess at 3&ndash;6 months.", "pbs": "All four are on the Lung Foundation 2026 chart (PBS-subsidised for COPD).", "prog": "Program Step 3, the top of Steps 1&ndash;3. <b>Refer if not stabilised, or worse, after 1 month.</b> Needing triple therapy (adding ICS) = refer. Week 6: eTG puts LAMA + LABA at Step 3; NICE uses it at Step 2 and adds ICS at Step 3.", "src": "QLD COPD + asthma protocols; eTG stable COPD; COPD-X 2.78; LFA Stepwise 2026; Week 6 Management Plan"},
{"id": "triple", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "ICS + LAMA + LABA triple therapy", "tag": "COPD (refer) · asthma Step 4", "agents": ["beclometasone+glycopyrronium+formoterol (Trimbow)", "budesonide+glycopyrronium+formoterol (Breztri Aerosphere)", "fluticasone furoate+umeclidinium+vilanterol (Trelegy Ellipta)", "mometasone+indacaterol+glycopyrronium (asthma, eTG)"], "moa": "Adds ICS to dual bronchodilation. In meta-analysis it improved lung function and quality of life, and reduced exacerbations, versus LAMA alone, LAMA+LABA or ICS+LABA (eTG).", "ci": "", "cautions": "Weigh the benefit against pneumonia and local effects: dysphonia, upper airway candidiasis (eTG). Response to oral corticosteroids does not predict response to ICS, so do not use it to choose patients.", "se": "ICS effects (pneumonia, dysphonia, candidiasis) plus LAMA and LABA effects.", "admin": "Single inhaler (eTG):<br>&bull; Trimbow 100+10+6 mcg, 2 puffs bd with spacer<br>&bull; Breztri 160+7.2+5 mcg, 2 puffs bd<br>&bull; Trelegy 100+62.5+25 mcg, 1 inhalation daily<br>Or ICS+LABA plus a separate LAMA. Assess at 3&ndash;6 months. Still symptomatic: refer to a respiratory physician.", "pbs": "Trelegy, Breztri and Trimbow are on the Lung Foundation 2026 chart (PBS-subsidised for COPD).", "prog": "<b>Not started under the Program:</b> needing triple = refer. Indications differ. <b>eTG:</b> 1 severe or &ge;2 moderate exacerbations in 12 months AND significant symptoms on dual. <b>LFA/COPD-X:</b> the same exacerbations plus raised eosinophils (eg &ge;300 cells/&micro;L) if available, or asthma features. eTG: one inhaler is no better than separate inhalers. COPD video: single-inhaler triple lowered mortality versus dual.", "src": "QLD COPD protocol; eTG stable COPD, asthma maintenance; LFA Stepwise 2026; COPD video 2"},
{"id": "ocs", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Oral corticosteroid &mdash; prednis(ol)one for flares", "tag": "asthma · COPD exacerbation", "agents": ["prednisolone", "prednisone"], "moa": "Systemic corticosteroid. In COPD flares it shortens hospital stay and speeds return to previous lung function and symptom control (eTG), and reduces severity and recovery time (LFA/COPD-X).", "ci": "", "cautions": "Treatment-harm risk factors: frequent oral corticosteroid use, and euphoria with it (eTG asthma). Cannot tolerate oral prednis(ol)one: emergency care (QLD COPD).", "se": "", "admin": "<b>COPD:</b> 30&ndash;50 mg once daily for 5 days (eTG). LFA: take in the morning with food, then stop.<br><b>Asthma</b> (eTG action plan): 37.5&ndash;50 mg daily for 5&ndash;10 days.<br>Short courses can stop abruptly without tapering (eTG).", "pbs": "", "prog": "Up to <b>5 days, no repeats</b>, and only enough tablets for that course, via the written action plan.<br><b>Asthma:</b> self-start if symptoms recur within 4 h or do not settle quickly AND no immediate medical review.<br><b>COPD:</b> self-start if symptoms persist or recur within 4 h of reliever.<br>See a doctor ASAP after starting. eTG asthma allows 5&ndash;10 days; the Program caps at 5.", "src": "QLD asthma + COPD protocols; eTG COPD exacerbations, asthma maintenance; World COPD Day 2020 (LFA)"},
{"id": "mont", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Montelukast (oral)", "tag": "asthma &mdash; do not initiate", "agents": ["montelukast"], "moa": "", "ci": "", "cautions": "Counsel on neuropsychiatric risk (TGA Safety Update, Jan 2025). Patients and carers should watch for behaviour changes and new psychiatric symptoms. Stop if they occur. Symptoms may persist after stopping (eTG).", "se": "Neuropsychiatric: behavioural changes, depression, suicidality, reported in all age groups. Usually mild, but can be serious (eTG).", "admin": "10 mg orally once daily (eTG). Less effective than regular low-dose ICS. eTG uses it instead of ICS (ICS adverse effects, steroid refusal, allergic rhinitis, inhalers impractical) or added to ICS.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>Pharmacist must not initiate</b> under the Program (Apr 2026). AAH: no role in routine adult or adolescent asthma. May continue therapy started by a respiratory specialist if the patient is eligible, with counselling. eTG still lists it as an alternative, but AAH prevails where they differ. Poor control on montelukast: switch to ICS or prn budesonide+formoterol (eTG).", "src": "QLD asthma protocol; summary of changes Apr 2026; eTG asthma maintenance"},
{"id": "abx", "grp": "Respiratory", "cls": "Antibiotics for COPD exacerbation", "tag": "COPD &mdash; referral only", "agents": ["amoxicillin", "doxycycline"], "moa": "The aim is faster recovery, not clearing airway colonisation, so doses are lower than for pneumonia (eTG).", "ci": "", "cautions": "Amoxicillin+clavulanate, macrolides and cephalosporins are not recommended first-line: no more effective, with broader-spectrum harms (eTG). Sputum colour alone is unreliable, and culture is not routine (QLD; eTG).", "se": "", "admin": "If indicated (eTG), one of:<br>&bull; amoxicillin 500 mg orally 8-hourly for 5 days<br>&bull; amoxicillin 1 g orally 12-hourly for 5 days<br>&bull; doxycycline 100 mg orally daily for 5 days<br>Use oral where possible. Not improving at 48&ndash;72 h rarely needs broader cover.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>Referral only:</b> refer suspected bacterial infection to a medical practitioner. The patient can stay in the Program with concurrent referral. Signs: more sputum or purulence, rapid breathing at rest, HR &gt;100, rigors. eTG: consider if purulence plus more volume and/or dyspnoea. COPD-X Handbook lets some action plans include antibiotic scripts; the Program requires referral.", "src": "QLD COPD + asthma protocols; eTG antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations; COPD-X Handbook; World COPD Day 2020"},
{"id": "acei", "grp": "Hypertension", "cls": "ACE inhibitors", "tag": "HTN · first-line · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["captopril", "enalapril", "fosinopril", "lisinopril", "perindopril arginine", "perindopril erbumine", "quinapril", "ramipril", "trandolapril"], "moa": "Reduce the synthesis of angiotensin-2 by inhibiting the action of ACE (HF 2016). Similar BP lowering to ARBs, but the two are <i>not necessarily interchangeable</i> in some conditions.", "ci": "<b>HF 2016 compelling:</b> pregnancy, angioedema, hyperkalaemia, bilateral renal artery stenosis. <b>Possible:</b> women with child-bearing potential.<br><b>eTG:</b> avoid with a history of angioedema; do not use in bilateral renal artery stenosis.", "cautions": "Sensitivity more likely with high-dose diuretics, hyponatraemia, renovascular hypertension, older age &mdash; start lower or lengthen the dosing interval.<br><b>Triple whammy</b> (ACEi/ARB + diuretic + NSAID incl. COX-2): acute kidney injury &mdash; avoid, esp. kidney disease, dehydration, older patients.<br>Not with an ARB (specialist only). Care with potassium-sparing diuretics (hyperkalaemia).", "se": "Cough; hyperkalaemia (risk up with renal impairment); renal impairment (risk up with hypovolaemia or NSAIDs); angioedema (infrequent; <b>may occur after years of treatment</b>). eTG: monitor for hypotension, kidney impairment and hyperkalaemia.", "admin": "Start low&ndash;moderate, increase slowly (eTG, daily unless stated): captopril 12.5&ndash;50 mg <b>twice daily</b>; enalapril 5&ndash;40 mg; fosinopril 10&ndash;40 mg; lisinopril 5&ndash;40 mg; perindopril arginine 5&ndash;10 mg; perindopril erbumine 4&ndash;8 mg; quinapril 5&ndash;40 mg; ramipril 2.5&ndash;10 mg; trandolapril 1&ndash;4 mg. Lower start in older patients or reduced kidney/liver function.", "pbs": "eTG (at time of writing): fixed-dose combination products are not PBS-listed for <i>initial</i> treatment of elevated BP &mdash; check the PBS website.", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> prescribable (max 2 first-line agents; combination products only if every component is prescribable). <b>Table 4:</b> eGFR + electrolytes <b>before and 1&ndash;2 weeks after</b> starting; a rise in creatinine <b>up to 25%</b> <b>or</b> potassium (within the normal range) should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation. BP review 4&ndash;6 weeks. No ARB combination unless specialist supervision.", "src": "eTG Hypertension; QLD HTN protocol V2 (H, Table 4); HF 2016 Tables 6.2, 6.3, 7.1; PHAR6302 Wk 2"},
{"id": "arb", "grp": "Hypertension", "cls": "Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs, sartans)", "tag": "HTN · first-line · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["candesartan", "eprosartan", "irbesartan", "losartan", "olmesartan", "telmisartan", "valsartan"], "moa": "Bind directly to the angiotensin-1 receptor, preventing its activation by angiotensin-2 (HF 2016). Similar cardioprotective and renoprotective effects to ACEi (eTG).", "ci": "<b>HF 2016 compelling:</b> pregnancy, angioedema, hyperkalaemia, bilateral renal artery stenosis. <b>Possible:</b> women with child-bearing potential.<br><b>eTG:</b> do not use in bilateral renal artery stenosis.", "cautions": "<b>Angioedema on an ACEi:</b> risk of the same with an ARB &mdash; harm&ndash;benefit analysis before prescribing (eTG); use cautiously (HF). Sensitivity more likely with high-dose diuretics, hyponatraemia, renovascular hypertension, older age. <b>Triple whammy</b> (ARB/ACEi + diuretic + NSAID): acute kidney injury. Not with an ACEi unless specialist. Care with potassium-sparing diuretics.", "se": "Hyperkalaemia (risk up with renal impairment); renal impairment (risk up with hypovolaemia or NSAIDs); cough and angioedema are rare. eTG: monitor for hypotension, kidney impairment and hyperkalaemia.", "admin": "Start low&ndash;moderate, increase slowly (eTG, daily): candesartan 8&ndash;32 mg; eprosartan 600 mg; irbesartan 150&ndash;300 mg; losartan 50&ndash;100 mg; olmesartan 20&ndash;40 mg; telmisartan 40&ndash;80 mg; valsartan 80&ndash;320 mg. Lower start in older patients or reduced kidney/liver function. (HF 2016 lists eprosartan 400&ndash;600 mg.)", "pbs": "eTG (at time of writing): fixed-dose combination products are not PBS-listed for <i>initial</i> treatment of elevated BP &mdash; check the PBS website.", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> prescribable. <b>Table 4:</b> eGFR + electrolytes before and 1&ndash;2 weeks later; creatinine rise <b>up to 25%</b> <b>or</b> potassium (within the normal range) should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation. Modifying an existing regimen: check for other indications (protocol example: <b>candesartan used for migraine prevention</b>) and work with the original prescriber.", "src": "eTG Hypertension; QLD HTN protocol V2 (H, Table 4); HF 2016 Tables 6.3, 7.1; PHAR6302 Wk 2"},
{"id": "dhpccb", "grp": "Hypertension", "cls": "Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers", "tag": "HTN · first-line · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["amlodipine", "felodipine modified-release", "lercanidipine", "nifedipine modified-release"], "moa": "Predominantly vasodilator action (eTG). Minimal effect on myocardial contractility and cardiac conduction (HF 2016).", "ci": "<b>HF 2016:</b> heart failure listed as a <i>possible</i> contraindication; no compelling contraindication listed.", "cautions": "Particularly useful with stable angina (eTG). Long-acting (once-daily) products preferred (HF 2016). Non-dihydropyridines (verapamil, diltiazem) are a different, second-line class &mdash; not interchangeable here.", "se": "Peripheral vasodilation (peripheral oedema, flushing, headache, dizziness), postural hypotension, tachycardia, palpitations, chest pain, gingival hyperplasia. Oedema is from fluid redistribution, not retention &mdash; <b>do not treat with diuretics</b>; reduce dose or stop if it does not subside (eTG).", "admin": "Start low&ndash;moderate, increase slowly (eTG, daily): amlodipine 5&ndash;10 mg; felodipine MR 5&ndash;20 mg; lercanidipine 10&ndash;20 mg; nifedipine MR 30&ndash;120 mg. Lower start in older patients. <b>HF 2016 differs:</b> amlodipine 2.5&ndash;10 mg; nifedipine CR 20&ndash;120 mg.", "pbs": "eTG (at time of writing): fixed-dose combination products are not PBS-listed for <i>initial</i> treatment of elevated BP &mdash; check the PBS website.", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> prescribable. <b>Table 4:</b> monitor for vasodilatory adverse effects &mdash; may require dose reduction. Pairs with ACEi/ARB (eTG; HF: particularly useful with diabetes and/or lipid abnormalities). BP review 4&ndash;6 weeks; <b>refer</b> if above target 4&ndash;6 weeks after two first-line agents at maximum tolerated doses.", "src": "eTG Hypertension; QLD HTN protocol V2 (Table 4, I); HF 2016 Tables 6.2, 6.3, 7.1; PHAR6302 Wk 2"},
{"id": "thiaz", "grp": "Hypertension", "cls": "Thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics", "tag": "HTN · first-line · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["chlortalidone", "hydrochlorothiazide", "indapamide", "indapamide modified-release"], "moa": "At BP-lowering doses: a predominantly vasodilator effect rather than a diuretic effect (eTG).", "ci": "<b>Gout</b> (eTG: avoid; HF 2016: compelling). HF 2016 compelling: <b>age</b> &mdash; first-line only in older patients (diabetes risk). <b>Possible:</b> glucose intolerance, metabolic syndrome, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia.", "cautions": "Not first-line in younger patients &mdash; associated with new-onset diabetes; may be first-line for uncomplicated elevated BP in patients older than 65 (eTG). Electrolyte, lipid and glucose effects are dose dependent (HF). Thiazide + beta-blocker not recommended with glucose intolerance, metabolic syndrome or diabetes (HF). The diuretic in the <b>triple whammy</b>.", "se": "Postural hypotension, dizziness, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia, hyperglycaemia (HF 2016; Wk 2). eTG: hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia are uncommon at BP-lowering doses.", "admin": "Start low, increase slowly (eTG, daily): chlortalidone 12.5&ndash;25 mg; hydrochlorothiazide 12.5&ndash;50 mg; indapamide 1.25&ndash;2.5 mg; indapamide MR 1.5 mg. <b>HF 2016:</b> hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily; indapamide CR 1.5 mg has similar effect to the 2.5 mg tablet with lower hypokalaemia risk; when uptitrating two drugs, exclude the thiazide.", "pbs": "eTG (at time of writing): fixed-dose combination products are not PBS-listed for <i>initial</i> treatment of elevated BP &mdash; check the PBS website.", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> prescribable. <b>Table 4:</b> plasma <b>sodium and potassium 3&ndash;6 weeks</b> after starting (eTG same); <b>refer</b> if outside reference intervals (RCPA Na 135&ndash;145, K 3.5&ndash;5.2 mmol/L). Pairs with ACEi/ARB (HF: useful with heart failure or post stroke). <b>Refer</b> if above target 4&ndash;6 weeks after two first-line agents at maximum tolerated doses.", "src": "eTG Hypertension; QLD HTN protocol V2 (Table 4, I); HF 2016 Tables 6.2, 6.3, 7.1, Fig 6.2; PHAR6302 Wk 2"},
{"id": "bb", "grp": "Hypertension", "cls": "Beta-blockers (role in hypertension)", "tag": "HTN · second-line · NOT QLD prescribable", "agents": ["atenolol", "metoprolol tartrate", "carvedilol", "labetalol", "nebivolol", "oxprenolol", "pindolol", "propranolol"], "moa": "", "ci": "<b>HF 2016 compelling:</b> asthma, bradycardia, A-V block (grade 2 or 3), uncontrolled heart failure. <b>Possible:</b> type 1 or 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, glucose intolerance, athletes and active patients, COPD (except vasodilator beta-blockers), depression.", "cautions": "<b>Avoid with verapamil or diltiazem</b> &mdash; heart block (eTG). HF 2016: verapamil avoid; diltiazem use with care (risk less than verapamil). Thiazide + beta-blocker not recommended with glucose intolerance, metabolic syndrome or diabetes (HF). Stopping suddenly: rebound sympathetic effects (high BP, tachyarrhythmia).", "se": "Bradycardia, postural hypotension, worsening of heart failure (transient), bronchospasm, cold extremities (HF 2016).", "admin": "eTG (start low, increase slowly): atenolol 25&ndash;100 mg daily; metoprolol tartrate 25&ndash;100 mg twice daily. <b>Stopping (eTG):</b> halve every 2&ndash;3 days to atenolol 25 mg daily or metoprolol 25 mg twice daily, then stop. <b>HF 2016:</b> stop slowly over &gt;2 weeks; atenolol not recommended as monotherapy. HF Table 7.1 also lists carvedilol, labetalol, nebivolol, oxprenolol, pindolol, propranolol.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>Not first-line</b> in uncomplicated HTN &mdash; less effective than first-line drugs at reducing stroke (eTG); less favourable efficacy&ndash;safety balance (HF 2016). Clear place in HFrEF, coronary artery disease/stable angina, after MI. <b>QLD:</b> not on the prescribable list (only ACEi, ARB, DHP CCB, thiazide). HF 2016: well-controlled HTN already on one &mdash; reasonable to continue.", "src": "eTG Hypertension; HF 2016 Tables 6.2, 6.3, 7.1; QLD HTN protocol V2 (H); PHAR6302 Wk 2"},
{"id": "statin", "grp": "Lipids", "cls": "Statins (HMG CoA reductase inhibitors)", "tag": "Lipids · first-line · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["atorvastatin", "fluvastatin", "pravastatin", "rosuvastatin", "simvastatin"], "moa": "HMG CoA reductase inhibitors (QLD Table 2). Benefit depends on the LDL-C reduction achieved, which depends on dose and potency; atorvastatin, rosuvastatin and, to a lesser extent, simvastatin are high-potency (eTG).", "ci": "", "cautions": "Higher adverse-effect risk (eTG): pre-existing muscle, liver or kidney disease; acute kidney injury (rosuvastatin); high dose/potency; drugs causing myopathy (colchicine, gemfibrozil) or inhibiting statin metabolism (amiodarone, diltiazem, clarithromycin, some antivirals); concurrent illness; frailty, advanced age. Deprescribing guide: can usually be stopped without tapering.", "se": "Muscle symptoms (causal link appears overestimated): avoid stopping outright &mdash; try another statin, lower dose or intermittent dosing; &gt;90% tolerate rechallenge. Rhabdomyolysis rare (CK typically &ge;10&times; ULN; never restart). Small rise in new-onset diabetes (outweighed by benefit). Mild ALT rise in 0.5&ndash;2%. No excess cognitive decline in RCTs.", "admin": "<b>High-intensity</b> = high dose of a high-potency statin: atorvastatin 40&ndash;80 mg; rosuvastatin 20&ndash;40 mg (CrCl &ge;30); simvastatin 80 mg (2nd choice, more myopathy). <b>Dose ranges</b> (low/medium start, titrate): atorvastatin 10&ndash;80; fluvastatin 80; pravastatin 20&ndash;80; rosuvastatin 5&ndash;40 (CrCl &lt;30: 5&ndash;10); simvastatin 10&ndash;80 mg daily. eTG gives no moderate-intensity list.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> first line unless contraindicated/not tolerated; <b>high dose of a high-potency statin</b> if high CVD risk or LDL-C &gt;4.0 mmol/L. Baseline LFT + CK, re-test if clinically indicated (eTG: CK only if muscle symptoms). Lipids at <b>6 weeks</b>; off target &rarr; adherence, uptitrate or switch to high potency, add ezetimibe. <b>Refer</b> if targets unmet 6 months after starting.", "src": "eTG Lipid modification; QLD Lipid protocol V2 (Tables 1&ndash;3, G); Deprescribing Statins guide + algorithm; PHAR6302 Wk 3"},
{"id": "ezet", "grp": "Lipids", "cls": "Ezetimibe", "tag": "Lipids · add-on or statin-intolerant · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["ezetimibe"], "moa": "", "ci": "", "cautions": "Cardiovascular outcome effects as monotherapy have only been studied in combination with a statin; effect in primary prevention not studied (eTG).", "se": "Muscle pain and mild ALT elevation &mdash; added to a statin, no increase beyond statin monotherapy. Routine ALT and CK monitoring not necessary (eTG).", "admin": "<b>ezetimibe 10 mg orally, daily.</b> Lowers LDL-C a further 20&ndash;25% added to a statin; 15&ndash;20% as monotherapy. Add if LDL-C target not met after 6 weeks of the maximum tolerated statin dose (eTG). After ACS with a statin: modest reduction in CV events over 7 years, no mortality reduction.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> prescribable; add if LDL-C targets not met on a statin; <b>may be monotherapy</b> if statins contraindicated or not tolerated (eTG: after reasonable attempts to reintroduce a statin). Max 2 agents; combination products only if all components prescribable. QLD Table 2 has no ezetimibe test row. Lipids at 6 weeks; refer if targets unmet at 6 months.", "src": "eTG Lipid modification; QLD Lipid protocol V2 (F, G); PHAR6302 Wk 3"},
{"id": "fenof", "grp": "Lipids", "cls": "Fenofibrate", "tag": "Lipids · triglyceride add-on · QLD prescribable", "agents": ["fenofibrate"], "moa": "", "ci": "Product information contraindicates use with eGFR &lt;30 mL/min; eTG notes it is used down to eGFR 20 mL/min in practice.", "cautions": "<b>With a statin:</b> product information warns against it, but studies show no myopathy increase beyond statin monotherapy &mdash; check CK regularly. <b>Gemfibrozil</b> (a different fibrate) significantly increases myositis with a statin &mdash; do not add it. No cardiovascular outcome benefit; not for LDL-C lowering unless all other options exhausted (eTG).", "se": "Paradoxical decrease in HDL, weeks to years after starting &mdash; consider stopping if markedly decreased from baseline (QLD Table 2).", "admin": "eTG: eGFR &gt;60 mL/min: 145 mg daily; eGFR 20&ndash;60: 48 mg daily, increase to 96 mg daily if no adverse effect on renal function. TG &gt;4 mmol/L: statin + fish oil, add fenofibrate if still raised; TG &gt;10: fenofibrate + fish oil. eTG: consider in diabetic retinopathy irrespective of lipids.", "pbs": "", "prog": "<b>QLD:</b> add-on for TG targets not met on statin + fish oil (fish oil is not on the prescribable list). <b>Table 2:</b> FBC + LFT before and 3-monthly in year 1; CK before and regularly with a statin; renal function &mdash; reduce dose if CrCl 30&ndash;60 (eTG uses eGFR 20&ndash;60). TG &ge;6 mmol/L or retinopathy = ineligible, refer.", "src": "eTG Lipid modification; QLD Lipid protocol V2 (F, G, Table 2, eligibility); PHAR6302 Wk 3"},
{"id": "nrt-patch", "grp": "Smoking cessation", "cls": "Nicotine transdermal patch (slow-acting NRT)", "tag": "Baseline nicotine; backbone of combination NRT", "agents": ["nicotine 21 mg/24 hours (preferred)", "nicotine 25 mg/16 hours", "lower strengths (eg 14 mg/24 h) for weaning or &lt;45 kg"], "moa": "Delivers nicotine through the skin at a relatively steady rate, relieving craving and withdrawal without the harmful components of tobacco smoke. Slow acting: peak effect 2 to 6 hours.", "ci": "Children &lt;12 years; known hypersensitivity to nicotine or any product component (RACGP). TG: seek specialist advice if hypersensitivity is suggested (vomiting, skin reactions, anaphylaxis after nicotine).", "cautions": "Recent (6 weeks) MI, unstable angina, severe arrhythmias or recent cerebrovascular event: use with caution, under medical supervision if the alternative is smoking. Stable CVD: safe. Recent skin graft (TG). &lt;45 kg may need lower maintenance dose. Pregnancy: 16-hour patch.", "se": "Skin irritation, redness, itch, rash (rotate site daily; 1% hydrocortisone if troublesome; adhesive irritation may need a brand change). Insomnia, vivid dreams or nightmares: change to the 16-hour patch or remove at bedtime.", "admin": "TG: 21 mg/24 h once daily unless pregnant (better morning-craving control); 25 mg/16 h removed at night for pregnancy or nightmares. Clean, dry, hairless skin; rotate site daily. Higher-dose patches preferred. Pre-quit patch for 2 weeks is a TGA-approved approach (RACGP).", "pbs": "PBS-subsidised as monotherapy with a support and counselling program; max 12 weeks of PBS NRT per 12 months; not at the same time as PBS varenicline or bupropion. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people: up to two 12-week courses a year.", "prog": "Use as part of combination NRT. Minimum course 12 weeks; review tolerability and effectiveness at 2 weeks, then at intervals. Offer a repeat course if wanted. Taper or stop abruptly per preference. Collaborate or refer: pregnancy, adolescents, breastfeeding, significant mental illness, important interactions, inadequate response.", "src": "eTG Nicotine replacement therapy (Jun 2023, amended Dec 2025); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (pharmacotherapy chapter); UoN PHAR6302 Week 5 course pages"},
{"id": "nrt-oral", "grp": "Smoking cessation", "cls": "Intermittent NRT: gum and lozenge (medium-acting), mist spray (fast-acting)", "tag": "Breakthrough and cue-driven cravings", "agents": ["nicotine 4 mg gum", "nicotine 4 mg lozenge", "nicotine 1 mg/spray mist spray"], "moa": "Acute nicotine doses for general and breakthrough craving; amount and timing titrated by the patient. Peak effect: gum 15 to 20 min, lozenge 20 min, mist spray under 10 min (TG).", "ci": "As for all NRT: children &lt;12 years; known hypersensitivity to nicotine or a product component (RACGP).", "cautions": "Phenylketonuria: use gum without artificial sweeteners (TG). Same cardiovascular cautions as other NRT. Pregnancy: if pharmacotherapy is needed, usually oral forms of NRT, with the pregnancy clinician consulted (RACGP).", "se": "Gum and lozenge: taste changes, throat irritation, hiccups, indigestion (TG); jaw discomfort, mouth irritation (Week 5). Mist spray: hiccups, increased salivation, throat irritation.", "admin": "Gum: chew to tingle, park in cheek, repeat up to 30 min. Lozenge: hold in cheek pouch, do not crunch. Both 1 to 2-hourly prn, max 16 per 24 h; strong craving: repeat after 30 min at the earliest. Spray: 1 to 2 sprays under tongue, max 4 per hour, 64 per 24 h. Avoid swallowing saliva.", "pbs": "Gum and lozenge PBS-subsidised only as the sole PBS therapy. Mist spray not PBS-subsidised but substantially cheaper than smoking (TG).", "prog": "Withdrawal symptoms on gum, lozenge or spray: check technique first. Nicotine inhalators are discontinued; switch to gum or lozenge, mist spray, or both (TG). Week 5: denture wearers wanting intermittent NRT is a practice scenario for using AMH and PSA guidance.", "src": "eTG Nicotine replacement therapy (Jun 2023, amended Dec 2025); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (pharmacotherapy chapter); UoN PHAR6302 Week 5 course pages"},
{"id": "nrt-combo", "grp": "Smoking cessation", "cls": "Combination NRT (patch plus oral form)", "tag": "First line for nicotine-dependent smokers", "agents": ["patch + gum", "patch + lozenge", "patch + mist spray"], "moa": "Patch gives a constant baseline; the oral form gives bursts for breakthrough craving, mimicking the nicotine pattern of smoking (TG). As effective as varenicline and more effective than single NRT (RACGP).", "ci": "As for NRT: children &lt;12 years; known nicotine hypersensitivity.", "cautions": "Highly dependent smokers (eg smoke within 30 min of waking) may need very large doses, even 2 or 3 patches plus oral forms (TG). If NRT is combined with bupropion, monitor blood pressure (RACGP).", "se": "Excess use: mild nicotine toxicity, most commonly dizziness, nausea, palpitations. Check whether still smoking; reduce the fast or medium-acting form before considering removing the patch (TG).", "admin": "Patch daily plus oral form as required, or regularly (eg hourly) ahead of triggers (RACGP). Minimum 12 weeks; longer, up to 6 months, more effective (TG).", "pbs": "Not PBS-subsidised as a combination: PBS covers one NRT form at a time, so add a nonsubsidised item. Prescribing only according to PBS funding underdoses (TG).", "prog": "Recommend for nicotine dependence (RACGP Recommendation 6, strong). NRT failure is usually inadequate dose or incorrect use. Review at 2 weeks; offer a repeat 12-week course regardless of reduction. Week 5: nausea, sweating, dizziness, palpitations at 1 week: assess (excess, still smoking, technique, illness, anxiety) before stopping.", "src": "eTG Nicotine replacement therapy (Jun 2023, amended Dec 2025); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (pharmacotherapy chapter); UoN PHAR6302 Week 5 course pages"},
{"id": "varenicline", "grp": "Smoking cessation", "cls": "Nicotinic receptor partial agonist", "tag": "Most effective monotherapy", "agents": ["varenicline"], "moa": "High-affinity <b>partial agonist</b> at nicotinic receptors: stimulates dopamine release (less than nicotine) to cut cravings and withdrawal, and blocks nicotine-induced dopamine release so smoking is less rewarding. As effective as combination NRT; more effective than bupropion or NRT alone (eTG).", "ci": "<b>Dialysis-dependent (end-stage) kidney disease</b> (eTG). Not recommended under 18; not first-line in pregnancy (eTG; RACGP: not recommended in pregnancy or breastfeeding).", "cautions": "History of <b>SJS/TEN</b> or severe skin hypersensitivity (eTG). eTG: earlier concerns about psychiatric effects and suicide risk <b>have not proved valid</b> &mdash; psychiatric comorbidity is not a contraindication, but review at 2 weeks. RACGP still advises reporting unusual mood change. No clinically meaningful interactions (RACGP).", "se": "Nausea in up to 30% &mdash; take with food. Insomnia &mdash; take the second dose mid-afternoon. Abnormal dreams. Withdrawal symptoms in week 1 if the partial agonism is not enough &mdash; smoking won&rsquo;t relieve them; stop and seek advice (eTG).", "admin": "<b>CrCl &ge;30:</b> 0.5 mg daily &times; 3 days, 0.5 mg bd &times; 4 days, then 1 mg bd for a 12-week course. <b>CrCl &lt;30:</b> 0.5 mg daily &times; 3 days, then 1 mg daily (eTG). Effects appear in 4&ndash;7 days; most stop smoking in weeks 1&ndash;2. Fixed or flexible quit date (RACGP).", "pbs": "Streamlined authority, up to 24 weeks continuous; requires a support and counselling program. Starter pack (4 weeks) plus 8-week continuation; a further 12 weeks only for those who respond (abstinent at 12 weeks).", "prog": "Review at <b>2 weeks</b> and <b>12 weeks</b>; offer a repeat 12-week course to anyone who wants it, whether or not smoking reduced (eTG). Medium/fast-acting NRT can be added (usually after 2 weeks) for cue-driven cravings. No evidence for varenicline + bupropion.", "src": "eTG Varenicline for tobacco smoking (2023); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation; PHAR6302 Week 5"},
{"id": "bupropion", "grp": "Smoking cessation", "cls": "Non-nicotine oral therapy (originally an antidepressant)", "tag": "First line, but less effective than varenicline", "agents": ["bupropion sustained-release"], "moa": "Noradrenaline and dopamine reuptake inhibitor and nicotinic receptor antagonist &mdash; reduces withdrawal and later cravings. First-line but less effective than combination NRT or varenicline (eTG).", "ci": "History of <b>seizures</b>; <b>eating disorders</b>; irreversible MAOIs (RACGP: MAOIs within 14 days, incl. moclobemide); <b>abrupt withdrawal from alcohol or benzodiazepines</b>; CNS tumours (Week 5). Not under 18; pregnancy only if NRT unsuitable (eTG).", "cautions": "Other drugs lowering seizure threshold (antipsychotics, antidepressants); doses &gt;300 mg/day not recommended (dose-dependent seizures). <b>Bipolar disorder</b> (mania). CYP450-metabolised &mdash; check interactions (eTG). Monitor BP if combined with NRT (RACGP).", "se": "Most common: <b>insomnia</b>, then dizziness, anxiety, nausea, dry mouth. Seizures rare but serious (about 0.1%).", "admin": "150 mg in the morning &times; 3 days, then 150 mg bd; review at 14 days; if effective and tolerated continue for a <b>9-week course</b>. Patients usually stop smoking in week 2 (eTG).", "pbs": "PBS: one 9-week course a year (eTG); streamlined authority with a support and counselling program (RACGP).", "prog": "Consider if varenicline/combination NRT not tolerated or ineffective, depression needs treating too, or a single drug is preferred. Review at 2 and 9 weeks. Medium/fast-acting NRT may be added after 2 weeks for cue-driven cravings. Offer a further course if wanted (eTG).", "src": "eTG Bupropion for tobacco smoking (2023); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation; PHAR6302 Week 5"},
{"id": "nvp", "grp": "Smoking cessation", "cls": "Nicotine vaping products (unapproved goods)", "tag": "Second line, after first-line treatment has failed", "agents": ["nicotine salt pods", "nicotine free-base liquid"], "moa": "Inhaled nicotine; dose varies with device, power, concentration, nicotine form (salt or free-base) and the length and intensity of inhalation. Users self-titrate (RACGP).", "ci": "Not recommended as a cessation aid under 18 years, or in pregnancy or breastfeeding (RACGP 2024).", "cautions": "Not registered: safety and quality not established; long-term effects unknown. Avoid dual use. Prefer closed systems; avoid free-base &gt;20 mg/mL (dilution and poisoning risk). Salt forms may be more addictive. NVP users have about twice the risk of relapse to smoking.", "se": "Free-base nicotine above 20 mg/mL causes adverse effects including throat irritation (RACGP).", "admin": "Start by dependence: salt pods 18 to 30 mg/mL (less dependent) or &gt;30 mg/mL (more); free-base 6 to 12 or 18 to 20 mg/mL. Max 3 months per prescription; avoid flavours where possible. Attempt weaning after 12 weeks; maximum 12 months is reasonable.", "pbs": "Not PBS. From 1 Oct 2024, S3: adults 18 and over may buy &le;20 mg/mL from a pharmacist without a prescription (identity and age sighted, max 1 month supply). Higher strengths or under 18 still need a prescription. Only TGA-notified vapes.", "prog": "RACGP Recommendation 15 (conditional): only after failed first-line therapy (behavioural support plus TGA-approved pharmacotherapy), via shared decision-making, with regular review. Refer to Quitline. Vaping cessation: behavioural support is key; NRT may be considered (Week 5).", "src": "RACGP NVPs and vaping cessation (Sep 2024); UoN PHAR6302 Week 5 course pages"},
{"id": "vax-flu", "grp": "Vaccines (chronic disease)", "cls": "Influenza vaccine", "tag": "Every year; COPD is an NIP-funded risk condition", "agents": ["standard-dose (egg- or cell-based)", "adjuvanted (Fluad; NIP-funded &ge;65 years)", "high-dose (Fluzone High-Dose)"], "moa": "", "ci": "Only absolute contraindications: anaphylaxis after a previous dose of any influenza vaccine, or after any vaccine component.", "cautions": "Egg allergy, even anaphylaxis, is not a barrier: give a full dose, do not split it. GBS unrelated to vaccination: vaccinate. GBS within 6 weeks of a vaccine: discuss risks and benefits.", "se": "Injection-site reactions (over 10%): induration, swelling, redness, pain. Fever, malaise, myalgia (1 to 10%), from a few hours, lasting 1 to 2 days. Influenza vaccines do not cause influenza.", "admin": "One dose each year, before the season (peaks June to September); never too late. &ge;65 years: adjuvanted or high-dose preferred over standard-dose. 50 to 64 years: standard-dose or adjuvanted.", "pbs": "NIP-funded: &ge;65 years; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people &ge;6 months; pregnant women; children 6 months to &lt;5 years; people &ge;6 months with specified conditions, including chronic respiratory conditions (COPD, chronic emphysema, severe asthma).", "prog": "COPD-X: actively promote annual influenza immunisation for COPD. QLD COPD protocol: check immunisation status (pneumococcal, RSV, COVID-19, influenza, pertussis, herpes zoster) and advise per the Handbook.", "src": "Australian Immunisation Handbook, Influenza (accessed 19 Oct 2026); COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024); QLD COPD Monitoring Program protocol"},
{"id": "vax-pneumo", "grp": "Vaccines (chronic disease)", "cls": "Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (21vPCV for adults)", "tag": "Single adult dose; COPD now an NIP-funded risk group", "agents": ["21vPCV (Capvaxive), adults", "20vPCV (Prevenar 20), under 18 years"], "moa": "", "ci": "Only absolute contraindications: anaphylaxis after a previous dose of any pneumococcal vaccine, or after any vaccine component.", "cautions": "Not routinely recommended in pregnancy; women with a risk condition ideally vaccinated before a planned pregnancy or soon after delivery. Error: 20vPCV given to an adult: give 21vPCV at least 2 months later.", "se": "Mostly mild to moderate: injection-site pain; fatigue and headache are the most common systemic events with 21vPCV.", "admin": "Single 0.5 mL IM dose of 21vPCV: all adults &ge;65; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander adults &ge;25; adults &ge;18 with a risk condition, at diagnosis. Previously vaccinated (including 23vPPV): give at least 12 months after the last dose. 23vPPV no longer required; need for further doses not established.", "pbs": "21vPCV NIP-funded: all adults &ge;65; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander adults &ge;25; adults &ge;18 with certain risk conditions, which now include COPD (chronic bronchitis, emphysema) and chronic liver disease.", "prog": "An adult who has 21vPCV for a risk condition at 18 to 64 needs no repeat at 65. COPD-X: encourage pneumococcal vaccination; check the Handbook and PneumoSmart tool for eligibility and funding.", "src": "Australian Immunisation Handbook, Pneumococcal disease (updated 30 Sep 2026); COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024)"},
{"id": "vax-rsv", "grp": "Vaccines (chronic disease)", "cls": "RSV vaccine (older adults)", "tag": "Single dose; COPD is a risk condition from 60 years", "agents": ["Arexvy", "Abrysvo"], "moa": "", "ci": "Only absolute contraindications: anaphylaxis after a previous dose of the same RSV vaccine, or after any vaccine component.", "cautions": "GBS: post-market excess cases (Abrysvo 9 to 18, Arexvy 5 to 7 per million doses within 42 days); now a rare AEFI in both PIs. Prior GBS unrelated to vaccination: discuss first. Arexvy must not be given in pregnancy (Abrysvo is the pregnancy vaccine).", "se": "Injection-site reactions (Abrysvo 12% vs 7% placebo in &ge;60 years). Rare adverse events of special interest include GBS and atrial fibrillation.", "admin": "Single dose; protection at least 2 years; revaccination not yet established. Any time of year, ideally before the season (April to September). May be co-administered with influenza, COVID-19, zoster and pneumococcal vaccines. No brand preference &ge;60; at 50 to 59 only Arexvy is registered.", "pbs": "Arexvy NIP-funded for all adults &ge;75 and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander adults &ge;60. Limited state and territory funding exists for selected older adults.", "prog": "Recommended: &ge;75; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander &ge;60; &ge;60 with a risk condition (eg COPD, chronic emphysema, severe asthma, diabetes, CKD eGFR &lt;30, BMI &ge;30). Can consider: 60 to 74 without risk factors; 50 to 59 with a risk factor.", "src": "Australian Immunisation Handbook, RSV (accessed 19 Oct 2026); QLD COPD Monitoring Program protocol"},
{"id": "vax-zoster", "grp": "Vaccines (chronic disease)", "cls": "Recombinant zoster vaccine (non-live, AS01B-adjuvanted)", "tag": "2 doses; no booster", "agents": ["Shingrix"], "moa": "", "ci": "Only absolute contraindications: anaphylaxis after a previous dose of Shingrix, or after any Shingrix component.", "cautions": "Not for preventing primary varicella or treating acute zoster or post-herpetic neuralgia. No pregnancy data. GBS within 6 weeks of vaccination: discuss. After Zostavax: wait at least 12 months (NIP: 5 years if the Zostavax was NIP-funded).", "se": "Injection-site reactions 82% (&ge;50 years); systemic (fever, fatigue, GI upset, headache, shivering, myalgia) 66%. About 10% have reactions disrupting daily activities, usually 1 to 3 days. Stress completing dose 2.", "admin": "2 x 0.5 mL IM, preferably deltoid. Immunocompetent &ge;50: 2 to 6 months apart. Immunocompromised &ge;18: 1 to 2 months apart. Dose 2 not repeated if the gap exceeds 6 months. After an episode: wait at least 12 months (immunocompetent), or from 3 months (immunocompromised).", "pbs": "NIP-funded: non-Indigenous people &ge;65; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people &ge;50; people &ge;18 with moderate or severe immunocompromise. Others by private prescription.", "prog": "QLD COPD protocol lists herpes zoster in the immunisation-status check. Can be given with other non-live vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal, RSV, COVID-19) at the same time or any time after.", "src": "Australian Immunisation Handbook, Zoster (updated 30 Sep 2026); QLD COPD Monitoring Program protocol"}
);
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["copd", "What does each letter of <b>COPD-X</b> stand for?", "<b>C</b> Case finding and confirm diagnosis<br><b>O</b> Optimise function<br><b>P</b> Prevent deterioration<br><b>D</b> Develop a plan of care<br><b>X</b> Manage eXacerbations<br>The action plan sits under <b>D</b>; pulmonary rehab and inhalers under <b>O</b>; smoking, vaccines, oxygen under <b>P</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Navigation and Summary of key recommendations.</div>"],
["copd", "Who should have spirometry to look for COPD, per the COPD-X Handbook?", "People <b>&ge;35 years</b> with one or more of: current or former smoking; new, persistent or changed cough; coughing up mucus; more breathless than others their age; chest tightness or wheeze; recurrent chest infections; a dust, gas or fume job.<br><b>Haemoptysis, chest pain and weight loss need urgent further investigation.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q2 Step 2.</div>"],
["copd", "What is the physical examination for in stable COPD, versus during an exacerbation?", "<b>Stable:</b> mainly to rule out alternative diagnoses; findings are often unremarkable.<br><b>Exacerbation:</b> tachypnoea, wheeze, accessory muscle use, tremor and cyanosis may give useful diagnostic information.<br>Clinical features or chest x-ray alone cannot diagnose COPD.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q2 Step 3 practice points.</div>"],
["copd", "When does the COPD-X Handbook suggest referral to specialist respiratory services?", "Diagnostic uncertainty; red flags (eg haemoptysis); rapid FEV<sub>1</sub> decline; frequent chest infections; onset of cor pulmonale; home oxygen assessment; bullous lung disease; COPD under 40 years; lung volume reduction or transplant assessment; unexplained breathlessness; suspected sleep disorder.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q2 Step 5.</div>"],
["copd", "When is <b>long-term oxygen</b> indicated in COPD, and what rules it out?", "Refer for assessment if SpO<sub>2</sub> is <b>below 90%</b>. Arterial blood gas, on air, at rest, awake:<br>&bull; PaO<sub>2</sub> consistently <b>&lt;55 mmHg</b> (SpO<sub>2</sub> &lt;88%), or<br>&bull; PaO<sub>2</sub> <b>&le;59 mmHg</b> plus polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure.<br>Use &gt;18 h/day. <b>Current smoking is an absolute contraindication</b> (fire, burns).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q7, recommendation 7b.</div>"],
["copd", "After an exacerbation, when should pulmonary rehabilitation start?", "As soon as practicable once acute instability has resolved. After a <b>hospitalised</b> exacerbation it is safe and should start <b>within 2-4 weeks of discharge</b>.<br>It improves exercise tolerance and quality of life, and reduces COPD admissions and short-term mortality.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q13, recommendation 13a.</div>"],
["copd", "What exercise target does the COPD-X Handbook give, and what happens after pulmonary rehab?", "Aim for <b>at least 150 minutes a week</b> of moderate to vigorous exercise (eg 30 min, 5 days); any activity beats none.<br>Maintenance: a community program such as <b>Lungs in Action</b>. If someone has stopped being active, reassess and consider <b>re-referral</b> to pulmonary rehab.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q4 non-pharmacological interventions.</div>"],
["copd", "What are the three zones of the Lung Foundation <b>My COPD Action Plan</b> (April 2026)?", "<b>I am well</b> (normal for me): usual activities, usual symptoms.<br><b>I am unwell</b> (flare-up): more breathless, using more reliever; activities harder; more cough; more sputum.<br><b>I am very unwell</b> (emergency): rapid worsening, very hard to breathe or talk, very anxious, confused or drowsy: <b>dial 000</b>.<br>Review the plan at least every 6-12 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Lung Foundation Australia, My COPD Action Plan (published April 2026).</div>"],
["copd", "On the April 2026 action plan, what are the three flare-up steps?", "<b>1</b> Use more reliever; keep using breathlessness techniques.<br><b>2</b> Rescue pack: worse breathlessness, take <b>prednisolone</b>; signs of infection (more sputum, change in colour, taste or consistency, with or without fever), take <b>antibiotics</b>.<br><b>3</b> If either is started, leave the clinic a message; not better after <b>2-3 days</b>, urgent same-day appointment. No rescue pack: go straight to 3.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Lung Foundation Australia, My COPD Action Plan (April 2026).</div>"],
["copd", "How often should a COPD action plan be reviewed, and what triggers an earlier review?", "At least <b>yearly</b> (COPD-X Handbook; the April 2026 plan says every 6-12 months).<br>Earlier if there have been <b>two flare-ups in the last 12 months</b> needing rescue medication (oral steroids and/or antibiotics). Patients record each flare-up so you can judge whether maintenance therapy needs changing.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q9 Figure 6, Steps 4 and 7; LFA My COPD Action Plan.</div>"],
["copd", "Moderate vs severe exacerbation: how do the LFA Stepwise chart and the QLD protocol define them?", "<b>Moderate</b> (both): needs systemic corticosteroids and/or antibiotics.<br><b>Severe</b>: LFA Stepwise 2026, hospitalisation <b>or ED presentation</b>; QLD protocol, hospitalisation.<br>QLD adds <b>mild</b>: short-acting bronchodilators, usually self-managed.<br>Escalate to triple therapy after &ge;1 severe or &ge;2 moderate in 12 months (LFA).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: LFA Stepwise Management of Stable COPD (June 2026); QLD COPD Monitoring Program protocol V2.</div>"],
["copd", "What review schedule does the LFA Stepwise Management chart (2026) set?", "&bull; Within <b>4-12 weeks of ANY change</b> (new inhaler; after any moderate or severe exacerbation; changes in lifestyle, comorbidities, adverse events, adherence or reliever use)<br>&bull; Then every <b>3-6 months</b> until stable<br>&bull; Then <b>at least annually</b>.<br>The QLD Program has its own review intervals; do not mix them.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: LFA Stepwise Management of Stable COPD (June 2026).</div>"],
["copd", "Name the differential diagnoses COPD-X lists for a suspected exacerbation.", "Pneumonia, pulmonary embolus, pneumothorax, malignancy, pleural effusion, heart failure, ischaemic heart disease, arrhythmia, anaemia, anxiety or depression.<br>Causes of a true exacerbation: <b>infectious</b> (viral, bacterial) or <b>non-infectious</b> (eg air pollution).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q10 Figure 7 (Celli 2023).</div>"],
["copd", "When does the COPD-X exacerbation algorithm say to send the patient to hospital?", "Any of: marked increase in symptoms; new or worsening peripheral oedema; hypoxaemia worse than usual; <b>SpO<sub>2</sub> &lt;92% if not on home oxygen</b>; breathlessness worsening or at rest; high fever; altered mental state; chest pain; worsening comorbidities; unable to manage safely at home; increased anxiety (feeling scared).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q11 Figure 8.</div>"],
["copd", "Oxygen and NIV in an exacerbation: flow rate, limits and the NIV trigger?", "Controlled oxygen <b>0.5-2.0 L/min</b> by nasal cannula, for hypoxaemia only; avoid over-oxygenation (acute respiratory failure, death). <b>Not</b> a treatment for breathlessness without hypoxaemia.<br>NIV for acute hypercapnic failure: <b>PaCO<sub>2</sub> &gt;45 mmHg and pH &lt;7.35</b>. Confusion, drowsiness, restlessness, cyanosis suggest respiratory failure.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q12.</div>"],
["copd", "Can COPD patients hold rescue-pack prescriptions, and what must they do when they start them?", "COPD-X: some patients with a written action plan and self-management education may hold scripts for <b>prednisolone 30-50 mg daily for 5 days</b> and <b>amoxicillin or doxycycline for 5 days</b>; they must arrange <b>early medical review</b> on starting.<br>QLD Program: suspected bacterial infection needing antibiotics means treat and <b>concurrently refer</b> to a medical practitioner.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q11 practice points; QLD COPD protocol V2.</div>"],
["copd", "Long-term macrolides and mucolytics in COPD: where do they stand in Australia?", "<b>Macrolides:</b> consider in moderate to severe COPD with frequent exacerbations, only after optimising everything else; <b>not PBS-listed</b> for long-term use; refer to a respiratory specialist.<br><b>Mucolytics</b> (N-acetylcysteine, erdosteine, carbocysteine, ambroxol) may reduce exacerbations but are <b>not available in Australia</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q7, recommendations 7a and 7d.</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd", "Under which COPD-X domain does the Handbook place writing a COPD action plan?", ["Optimise function", "Prevent deterioration", "Develop a plan of care", "Manage eXacerbations"], 2, "Clinical question 9 (role of the action plan and how to write one) sits under <b>D: Develop a plan of care</b>, although the plan is used to manage exacerbations (X).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Navigation.</div>"],
["copd", "A stable COPD patient breathing air at rest has a PaO<sub>2</sub> of 57 mmHg. There is no polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure. Is long-term oxygen indicated?", ["Yes, because any PaO<sub>2</sub> below 60 mmHg qualifies", "No, the criteria are not met", "Yes, provided the SpO<sub>2</sub> is under 92% at rest", "No, oxygen is only used during exacerbations"], 1, "Criteria: PaO<sub>2</sub> consistently <b>&lt;55 mmHg</b>, or <b>&le;59 mmHg plus</b> polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure. 57 mmHg alone meets neither.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q7, recommendation 7b.</div>"],
["copd", "A patient with COPD and resting hypoxaemia who still smokes asks about home oxygen. What does the COPD-X Handbook say?", ["Current smoking is an absolute contraindication to oxygen therapy", "Oxygen can start once a quit date is set", "Oxygen is fine if limited to under 18 hours a day", "Only an e-cigarette user is excluded"], 0, "The Handbook states oxygen therapy is an <b>absolute contraindication</b> in people currently smoking, because of the risk of fire and burns.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q7 practice points.</div>"],
["copd", "A patient using the April 2026 My COPD Action Plan started their rescue-pack prednisolone and antibiotics this morning. What does the plan tell them to do next?", ["Wait until the full 5-day course is finished before contacting anyone", "Go straight to the emergency department for assessment today", "Double the prednisolone dose if not feeling better by tonight", "Message the clinic; same-day appointment if no better in 2-3 days"], 3, "Step 3 of the <b>I am unwell</b> zone: if either rescue medicine is started, leave a message with the clinic; if not getting better after 2-3 days, arrange an urgent (same-day) appointment. Emergency signs mean 000.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Lung Foundation Australia, My COPD Action Plan (April 2026).</div>"],
["copd", "On the LFA Stepwise Management chart (2026), a COPD flare treated in the emergency department without admission counts as what?", ["A mild exacerbation", "A severe exacerbation", "A moderate exacerbation", "Not an exacerbation"], 1, "LFA: <b>severe</b> = acute worsening requiring hospitalisation <b>or emergency department presentation</b>; moderate = needing systemic corticosteroids and/or antibiotics. The QLD protocol defines severe as requiring hospitalisation, so name the framework you are using.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: LFA Stepwise Management of Stable COPD (June 2026); QLD COPD protocol V2.</div>"],
["copd", "A patient is discharged after a hospital admission for a COPD exacerbation. When should pulmonary rehabilitation start?", ["Only after 3 months of stability", "Once spirometry has returned to baseline", "Within 2-4 weeks of discharge", "Pulmonary rehab is unsafe after a recent admission"], 2, "After a hospitalised exacerbation pulmonary rehab is <b>safe</b> and should start <b>within 2-4 weeks of discharge</b>, as soon as acute instability has resolved.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q13 practice points.</div>"],
["copd", "Which finding needs urgent further investigation rather than routine case-finding spirometry?", ["Persistent productive cough in a 50-year-old ex-smoker", "Haemoptysis", "Recurrent chest infections", "Breathless more easily than others the same age"], 1, "The others are listed reasons to arrange spirometry for people &ge;35. <b>Haemoptysis, chest pain and weight loss</b> require urgent further investigation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia 2024), Q2 Step 2.</div>"],
["copd", "Under the LFA Stepwise Management chart (2026), a patient has just started a new maintenance inhaler. When should they be reviewed?", ["Within 1 week", "Within 4-12 weeks", "At 6 months", "At the next annual review"], 1, "Review <b>within 4-12 weeks of any change</b> (eg new inhaler, after a moderate or severe exacerbation), then every 3-6 months until stable, then at least annually.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: LFA Stepwise Management of Stable COPD (June 2026).</div>"]
);


/* ---------- T. Diabetes diagnosis/monitoring (eTG, QLD CCM, Diabetes Australia, quick guide, DDS) + KHA CKD Handbook 5th ed (19 Oct 2026) ----------
   Agent-drafted; all evidence quotes machine-checked (drafts/diabetes_sources.json, drafts/kha_ckd.json). */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["bgl", "Two different diagnostic tests disagree &mdash; one is above the diabetes threshold, the other is not. What do you do?", "Repeat the test that was <b>above</b> the diagnostic threshold; the diagnosis can then be made on the confirmed test.<br>An HbA1c below 48 mmol/mol (6.5%) does <b>not</b> refute a diagnosis made on venous glucose or an OGTT.<br>Meeting a prediabetes threshold on one test does not refute diabetes diagnosed on another.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Can a finger-prick (capillary) glucose diagnose diabetes? Is there an exception?", "<b>No</b> &mdash; diagnose on venous samples tested by validated laboratory methods; do not use point-of-care capillary testing to diagnose.<br><b>Exception &mdash; suspected type 1:</b> do not wait for a fasting glucose or HbA1c. A finger-prick glucose or urine dipstick can be used initially; elevated glucose, or significant glycosuria with ketonuria, is a medical emergency needing immediate specialist assessment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "A teenager presents with hyperglycaemia. How urgent is it, and which type of diabetes is assumed?", "A <b>medical emergency</b>, with or without symptoms. Children and adolescents are presumed to have, and are treated for, <b>type 1 diabetes</b> unless specialist assessment proves otherwise. Refer to the local emergency department, or phone the nearest children&rsquo;s hospital or major healthcare service for specialist endocrinology advice &mdash; the risk is DKA from acute insulin deficiency.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Give the typical presenting features that separate type 1 from type 2 diabetes (eTG Table 18.1).", "<b>Type 1:</b> peak in adolescents and young adults (but any age); acute polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss; ketosis often present; own or family history of autoimmune disease may be present.<br><b>Type 2:</b> incidence rises with age; often asymptomatic or mild symptoms (eg fatigue); obesity and other CVD risk factors; higher-risk groups include Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander, Māori, Pacific and southern Asian peoples.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Name four reasons the type of diabetes can be misjudged at diagnosis.", "&bull; Type 1 can present at any age (CCM example: latent autoimmune diabetes in adults, LADA).<br>&bull; About one-quarter of people with type 1 are overweight at diagnosis.<br>&bull; DKA, a hallmark of type 1, can occur in type 2 (uncommon).<br>&bull; Some people with type 1 lack the associated autoantibodies &mdash; do not rule out type 1, or immediate insulin, on that basis.<br>Seek expert advice when unclear.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026); QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "An HbA1c is reported as 47 mmol/mol. Diabetes, prediabetes, or neither?", "The accepted thresholds (prediabetes 42–46, diabetes &ge;48 mmol/mol) do <b>not</b> define 47. Australian laboratories dual-report, and 47 mmol/mol is generally rounded up to <b>6.5%</b> &mdash; the diabetes threshold. In an asymptomatic patient, any result at or above the threshold still needs confirming on a different day.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Asymptomatic adult, HbA1c 44 mmol/mol (6.2%). What further testing does eTG suggest?", "This is the prediabetes range (42–46 mmol/mol, 6.0–6.4%). Further testing with an <b>OGTT or a fasting venous glucose</b> can detect diabetes. An OGTT can be considered if diagnosing impaired glucose tolerance would change management &mdash; eg general lifestyle modification versus a structured, intensive lifestyle intervention. Prediabetes needs more frequent follow-up of CVD risk factors and diabetes risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "When is an OGTT the right test, and when should it never be used?", "<b>Use:</b> it is the only test that detects impaired glucose tolerance; when fasting glucose alone may underdiagnose (eg polycystic ovary syndrome, glucocorticoid use); to confirm gestational diabetes; postpartum after hyperglycaemia in pregnancy.<br><b>Never:</b> to diagnose type 1 diabetes, or when diagnostic thresholds are already met on other tests.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "How must a 75 g OGTT be performed in a non-pregnant adult?", "&bull; Fast overnight 8–12 h; no smoking or caffeine; water allowed until the glucose drink. No carbohydrate-loading diet.<br>&bull; Start before 10 am; fasting venous sample just before the drink, which is finished within 5 minutes.<br>&bull; Stay seated; nothing to eat or drink, including water, for 2 h.<br>&bull; Venous sample at 2 h. Bring food for afterwards &mdash; some go hypo or feel lightheaded.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Hyperglycaemia during an acute illness or on high-dose prednisolone: how do you separate it from undiagnosed diabetes?", "Acute stressors (infection, cardiovascular event, surgery) and medicines (eg high-dose glucocorticoids) can precipitate hyperglycaemia. An <b>HbA1c</b> may help gauge how long it has been present. Do definitive testing <b>after the acute event resolves</b>. Even if glucose returns to normal, the person should be considered at greater risk of developing diabetes.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Where do the QLD Chronic Conditions Manual (CCM) and eTG diagnostic tables disagree?", "&bull; <b>Fasting prediabetes:</b> CCM 5.5–6.9 mmol/L; eTG 6.1–6.9 (adults), 5.6–6.9 (children).<br>&bull; <b>Random glucose:</b> CCM labels 7.9–11.0 prediabetes; eTG defines no random prediabetes range, but suggests further testing at 7.8–11.<br>&bull; <b>Repeat rule:</b> CCM confirms if asymptomatic and close to normal; eTG confirms any asymptomatic positive.<br>Diabetes cut-offs agree: &ge;48 mmol/mol, fasting &ge;7.0, random or 2 h OGTT &ge;11.1.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Who does the QLD Chronic Conditions Manual list as high risk for diabetes?", "Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, Middle Eastern, Asian and Māori people &bull; smoking, inactivity, overweight or obesity &bull; metabolic syndrome &bull; low birth weight or large-for-gestational-age babies &bull; history of GDM or polycystic ovary syndrome &bull; cardiovascular or cerebrovascular disease &bull; psychosocial stress, depression or antipsychotics &bull; personal or family history of diabetes or autoimmune conditions.<br>AUSDRISK &ge;12: confirm with a blood test.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "A woman with diabetes risk factors becomes pregnant. When is she screened, and what HbA1c target applies around pregnancy?", "<b>CCM:</b> screen for GDM (or undiagnosed type 2) when pregnancy is confirmed &mdash; <b>6–12 weeks</b> &mdash; and if normal, repeat at <b>24–28 weeks</b>. Aim for HbA1c &le;6.5% (48 mmol/mol) without severe hypoglycaemia before conception and during pregnancy; offer contraception until reached.<br><b>eTG:</b> do not use HbA1c to diagnose or monitor in the first 3 months postpartum &mdash; falsely low.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Who needs self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG)? Compare the sources.", "&bull; <b>Quick guide:</b> everyone on a sulfonylurea and/or insulin needs daily SMBG; for others it is individual (eg starting steroids, sick days).<br>&bull; <b>QLD protocol:</b> encourage SMBG during treatment changes or intensive lifestyle modification.<br>&bull; <i>NHMRC 2009 &mdash; dated:</i> consider in all; individualise frequency and timing.<br>Everyone with diabetes needs regular HbA1c, whatever the treatment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022); QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2; NHMRC National Evidence-Based Guideline for Blood Glucose Control in Type 2 Diabetes (2009 &mdash; dated, superseded)</div>"],
["bgl", "How many BGL checks a day suit each regimen (Diabetes quick guide)?", "&bull; Not on a sulfonylurea or insulin: 0–1, random<br>&bull; Sulfonylurea: 1–2, fasting + random<br>&bull; Basal insulin only: 1–2, fasting + random<br>&bull; Basal-plus or premix: 2–4, fasting + before bolus<br>&bull; Basal-bolus, incl. type 1 and pumps: 4+<br><i>Random</i> = a different time each day; repeat that time next day if out of target. Overnight 2–3 am occasionally on basal insulin.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022)</div>"],
["bgl", "When should extra BGL checks be done, and what level is needed to drive?", "Extra checks: before driving, during a hypo, before and after extra activity, when unwell (infection, diarrhoea, vomiting), and whenever concerned.<br>Driving: BGL must be <b>above 5.0 mmol/L</b>. CCM adds: notify the road traffic authority of fitness to drive, and annual physician or endocrinologist review for licence holders on hypoglycaemic agents.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Australia &mdash; Blood glucose monitoring (web page, Oct 2026); Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022); QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "Give the SMBG target ranges, and where the sources differ (all mmol/L).", "<b>QLD protocol action plan / Diabetes Australia:</b> 4.0–7.0 fasting or before meals; 5.0–10 two hours after.<br><b>CCM:</b> type 2 the same; type 1 and adults 18–30: 4–6 fasting, 4–8 after; elderly or comorbid: 6–8 and 6–12.<br><b>Quick guide:</b> 4.0–8.0 before meals; 6.0–10.0 after and overnight.<br><i>NHMRC 2009 &mdash; dated:</i> 6–8 and 6–10.<br>Program patients: quote the protocol.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2; Diabetes Australia &mdash; Blood glucose target range (web page, Oct 2026); QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022); NHMRC National Evidence-Based Guideline for Blood Glucose Control in Type 2 Diabetes (2009 &mdash; dated, superseded)</div>"],
["bgl", "How often should HbA1c be checked? Compare the sources.", "<b>QLD protocol Table 3:</b> 3-monthly if newly diagnosed, changing therapy or off target; 6-monthly once stable at target.<br><b>CCM:</b> 6-monthly; more often if target not met.<br><b>Quick guide:</b> not on a sulfonylurea or insulin, 6–12-monthly; on a sulfonylurea and/or insulin, 3–6-monthly; 3-monthly if hyperglycaemic or above target.<br>HbA1c reflects roughly the last 2–3 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2; QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022)</div>"],
["bgl", "Where is the QLD Chronic Conditions Manual cycle of care stricter than the Blood Glucose protocol&rsquo;s Table 3?", "&bull; <b>Eyes:</b> CCM retinal exam 12-monthly; protocol at least every 2 years.<br>&bull; <b>Dental:</b> 6-monthly vs 12.<br>&bull; <b>Weight, waist:</b> 3-monthly vs 6.<br>&bull; <b>Emotional wellbeing:</b> 12-monthly vs as needed.<br>&bull; <b>Feet:</b> CCM every visit; protocol 1–12 months by risk.<br>CCM also: eGFR and ACR 3-monthly if abnormal; ECG 12-monthly. Program patients: quote the protocol.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2</div>"],
["bgl", "What is the DDS2, and how is diabetes distress scored?", "DDS2: two items, each rated 1 (not a bother) to 6 (very bothersome) for the <b>past month</b> &mdash; feeling <b>overwhelmed</b> by the demands of living with diabetes, and feeling <b>often failing</b> with the diabetes routine.<br>The scoring sheet supplied is for the DDS17: a <b>mean item score &ge;3</b> (moderate distress) is worthy of clinical attention; discuss any single item scored &ge;3. No separate DDS2 cut-off is given.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Distress Screening Scale (DDS2 and DDS17 form with scoring sheet; CCM Resource 5)</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient says their meter gives different readings. What do you check, and when is a difference a concern?", "Every meter gives a different result with a different drop; a gap of <b>2 mmol/L or less</b> is not usually a concern. Check: strips in date and right for the meter, enough blood, strip inserted correctly, strips stored dry, hands washed and dried (sweet foods raise results), meter clean, temperature, battery. A pharmacist or credentialled diabetes educator can check the meter with control solution.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Australia &mdash; Blood glucose monitoring (web page, Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "How does the CCM define and describe hypoglycaemia, and what raises the risk?", "BGL <b>&lt;4.0 mmol/L</b> &mdash; shaking, sweating, racing pulse, confusion, irritability, hunger or tiredness. CCM lists hypoglycaemia as an urgent referral (Primary Clinical Care Manual). Monitor closely with sulfonylureas and insulins; inconsistent food intake raises hypo risk on some of these; alcohol lowers glucose and masks hypo symptoms &mdash; avoid binge drinking and eat when drinking.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "How is prediabetes followed up in the QLD Chronic Conditions Manual?", "Intensive lifestyle modification aiming for <b>5–10% weight loss</b> if overweight or obese. <b>Annual HbA1c or OGTT</b> &mdash; reduce frequency if results do not deteriorate and lifestyle has improved. Prediabetes cycle of care: random BGL 3-monthly; fasting BGL, OGTT or HbA1c and CVD risk calculator 12-monthly; dietitian 3-monthly. eTG: prediabetes thresholds do not apply to type 1.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["htn", "Who should be offered a Kidney Health Check, how often, and what does it involve? (KHA, non-Indigenous adults)", "<b>Annually:</b> diabetes, hypertension.<br><b>Every 2 years:</b> established CVD, family history of kidney failure, obesity (BMI &ge;30), smoking/vaping.<br><b>History of AKI:</b> yearly for 3 years, then every 2 years.<br><b>Aged &ge;60:</b> once off, unless other indications develop.<br><b>First Nations Australians &ge;18:</b> at least annually.<br><b>The check:</b> BP + uACR (first morning void preferred) + eGFR.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.12&ndash;15.</div>"],
["htn", "How does the QLD Program decide when to order uACR and eGFR, and how does that differ from KHA?", "QLD (stated as based on KHA): test if <b>not done in the previous 12 months</b> in T2D, hypertension, family history of CKD, history of AKI, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander &ge;18, obese BMI, current/past smoker or vaper, aged &ge;60.<br><b>Difference:</b> KHA staggers frequency &mdash; annual only for diabetes, hypertension and First Nations adults; 2-yearly for the others; once off at &ge;60. KHA also lists established CVD.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.14; QLD Hypertension protocol, uACR and eGFR row.</div>"],
["htn", "How does KHA define chronic kidney disease?", "Either:<br>&bull; eGFR (or measured GFR) <b>&lt;60 mL/min/1.73m&sup2; for &ge;3 months</b>, with or without kidney damage; <b>or</b><br>&bull; evidence of kidney damage for <b>&ge;3 months</b>, with or without reduced GFR &mdash; albuminuria, haematuria after urological causes are excluded, structural (imaging) or pathological (biopsy) abnormalities.<br>CKD is usually silent: up to 90% of kidney function may be lost before symptoms.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.11.</div>"],
["htn", "A first eGFR comes back at 52 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;. What does KHA say to do before calling it CKD?", "Retest <b>within 7 days</b> to exclude AKI, then again <b>after 3 months</b> to establish CKD. If the repeat shows a <b>&ge;20% reduction</b>, consider possible AKI and discuss with a nephrologist.<br>Do not be reassured by a normal creatinine: 50% or more of kidney function can be lost before serum creatinine rises above the upper limit of normal.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.16&ndash;18.</div>"],
["htn", "How is albuminuria confirmed, and which sample is preferred?", "A positive uACR (<b>&ge;3.0 mg/mmol</b>) is repeated within 3 months; if the repeat is normal, do a third test. Albuminuria is present if <b>at least two of the results are &ge;3.0 mg/mmol</b> over 3 months.<br><b>First morning void</b> is preferred &mdash; protein excretion peaks in the afternoon; a random spot sample is acceptable. Urine dipstick for protein is <b>no longer recommended</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.16, 19.</div>"],
["htn", "Give the KHA CKD staging bands for eGFR and albuminuria.", "<b>eGFR (mL/min/1.73m&sup2;):</b> 1 &ge;90 &bull; 2 60&ndash;89 &bull; 3a 45&ndash;59 &bull; 3b 30&ndash;44 &bull; 4 15&ndash;29 &bull; 5 &lt;15 or dialysis.<br><b>uACR:</b> A1 &lt;3.0 &bull; A2 3.0&ndash;30 &bull; A3 &gt;30 mg/mmol (one set of figures, not sex-specific).<br>Stage 1&ndash;2 with A1 is not CKD unless haematuria, structural or pathological abnormality. Fully specify, e.g. <i>CKD stage 3b with A2 in the presence of type 2 diabetes</i>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.16, 21.</div>"],
["htn", "Which KHA colour-coded action plan applies, and how often is the person reviewed?", "<b>Yellow</b> &mdash; eGFR &ge;60 with A2, or eGFR 45&ndash;59 with A1: review <b>every 12 months</b>.<br><b>Orange</b> &mdash; eGFR 30&ndash;59 with A2, or eGFR 30&ndash;44 with A1: <b>every 3&ndash;6 months</b>.<br><b>Red</b> &mdash; macroalbuminuria (A3) at any eGFR, or eGFR &lt;30 regardless of albuminuria: <b>every 1&ndash;3 months</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.26&ndash;28.</div>"],
["htn", "What BP target does KHA set in CKD, and why must you not carry it into the QLD Program?", "<b>KHA:</b> consistently <b>below 130/80 mmHg for all people with CKD</b>; SBP &lt;120 may suit some at very high CV risk, balanced against falls, electrolyte abnormalities and AKI.<br><b>QLD protocol:</b> targets come from the risk band &mdash; 140/90 (low/intermediate) or SBP 120 (high). Stage 3&ndash;5 CKD or persistent albuminuria makes a patient ineligible anyway.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.9, 52; QLD Hypertension protocol.</div>"],
["htn", "After starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB, how much eGFR fall does KHA accept &mdash; and how does the QLD rule differ?", "<b>KHA:</b> check eGFR within 2 weeks. A fall of <b>less than 25%</b> &rarr; continue. <b>More than 25% below baseline</b> &rarr; cease and consider nephrology referral.<br><b>QLD protocol:</b> a <b>creatinine rise</b> up to 25% <b>or</b> potassium within the normal range should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation.<br>Different measures (eGFR fall vs creatinine rise) &mdash; quote each as written.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.34, 55; QLD Hypertension protocol Table 4.</div>"],
["htn", "Should an ACE inhibitor or ARB be stopped as CKD progresses?", "<b>No.</b> KHA: they can be safely prescribed at all stages of CKD and should <b>not be ceased as GFR progressively declines</b> &mdash; continue if tolerated.<br>They are first-line for all people with CKD; up-titrate to the <b>maximum tolerated dose</b> to reduce albuminuria and kidney function decline.<br>Combined ACE inhibitor + ARB is <b>not recommended</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.34, 55.</div>"],
["htn", "How does KHA handle potassium in a person with CKD on an ACE inhibitor or ARB?", "Expect a rise of about <b>0.5 mmol/L</b>; take care if baseline K+ is <b>&ge;5.5</b>. Target K+ <b>&le;6.0 mmol/L</b>.<br><b>6.0&ndash;6.5:</b> low-K+ diet (dietitian), avoid salt substitutes, correct acidosis, consider a thiazide or a binder; cease ACEi/ARB/MRA if persistently &gt;6.0 despite these.<br><b>&gt;6.5:</b> refer to the nearest Emergency Department (arrhythmia risk).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.54&ndash;55, 65.</div>"],
["htn", "How does KHA define the triple whammy, and what counselling does it ask for?", "RAS blockade (ACE inhibitor or ARB) + diuretic + NSAID or COX-2 inhibitor (<b>except low-dose aspirin</b>) can cause AKI &mdash; especially if the patient is volume-depleted or has CKD.<br><b>Action:</b> make sure anyone on an ACE inhibitor or ARB <b>plus</b> a diuretic knows to discuss pain relief with a GP or pharmacist before taking it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.36.</div>"],
["htn", "Spell out SADMANS and the two reasons each drug is withheld on a sick day.", "<b>S</b>ulfonylureas &bull; <b>A</b>CE inhibitors &bull; <b>D</b>iuretics &bull; <b>M</b>etformin &bull; <b>A</b>RBs &bull; <b>N</b>SAIDs &bull; <b>S</b>GLT2 inhibitors.<br><b>Kidney function decline:</b> ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs, diuretics.<br><b>Adverse events:</b> metformin, sulfonylureas, SGLT2 inhibitors.<br>Withhold temporarily if unable to maintain fluid intake (especially sepsis, hypovolaemia, hypotension); restart when stable.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.33, 35, 41.</div>"],
["bgl", "SGLT2 inhibitors in CKD &mdash; who, the initiation floor, and what to expect for eGFR?", "Recommended in CKD with proteinuria, <b>with or without diabetes</b>. Not recommended to <b>initiate if eGFR &lt;25</b>.<br>A reversible eGFR drop is expected, <b>greatest at 4 weeks</b>, then rebounds; KHA says specific eGFR testing for this is not required. Osmotic diuresis: consider reducing diuretics/antihypertensives at initiation.<br><b>QLD protocol:</b> check eGFR and creatinine before, then at least annually; a 3&ndash;5 fall usually stabilises.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.34; QLD Blood Glucose protocol Table 2.</div>"],
["bgl", "How does KHA dose metformin by eGFR, and how does it compare with the QLD monitoring rule?", "<b>KHA:</b> eGFR &gt;60 &mdash; maximum dose required, kidney function annually. eGFR 30&ndash;60 &mdash; consider reducing the dose; monitor every 3&ndash;6 months. eGFR &lt;30 &mdash; <b>only under specialist supervision</b>. Stop temporarily during illness (lactic acidosis).<br><b>QLD protocol:</b> eGFR before starting, then <b>every 4&ndash;6 months</b>, plus B12 yearly.<br>Either way, eGFR &lt;60 is a Program referral trigger.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.35, 50; QLD Blood Glucose protocol Table 2.</div>"],
["bgl", "What glycaemic targets does KHA set in CKD, and why does hypoglycaemia risk rise?", "<b>HbA1c:</b> generally &le;53 mmol/mol (range 48&ndash;58); &le;7% (range 6.5&ndash;7.5), individualised.<br><b>BGL:</b> 6&ndash;8 mmol/L fasting; 8&ndash;10 mmol/L postprandial.<br>Hypoglycaemia becomes more frequent as eGFR declines &mdash; reduced clearance of endogenous and injected insulin.<br>HbA1c can mislead: iron deficiency raises it, anaemia lowers it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.49.</div>"],
["lipid", "When does KHA recommend a statin in CKD, and what does it say about lipid targets?", "Statin (&plusmn; ezetimibe) for CKD with <b>eGFR &ge;15</b> and <b>CVD risk &ge;10%</b>; for <b>First Nations Australians</b> with CKD and <b>CVD risk &ge;5%</b>.<br><b>No target lipid level</b>, and follow-up lipid measurement is not required for most patients.<br><b>QLD lipid protocol differs:</b> risk-based LDL-C targets, review at 6 weeks, refer if not at target by 6 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.9, 65; QLD Dyslipidaemia protocol.</div>"],
["lipid", "What lipid pattern is typical in CKD, and when does KHA suggest specialist evaluation?", "Hypertriglyceridaemia with <b>low HDL</b> but <b>normal LDL</b>; worse with albuminuria, particularly nephrotic syndrome.<br>Do a fasting lipid profile in newly identified CKD. Consider secondary causes and specialist evaluation if <b>LDL-C &gt;4.9</b> or <b>TG &gt;11.3 mmol/L</b>.<br>Compare: QLD Program referral thresholds are LDL-C &ge;5.0 and TG &ge;6 mmol/L.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.65; QLD Hypertension protocol step C.</div>"],
["htn", "How does CKD change CVD risk under KHA, and how does the QLD wording compare?", "<b>KHA:</b> eGFR <b>&lt;45</b> or uACR <b>&gt;30</b> = pre-determined high risk (&ge;10% in 5 years). eGFR 45&ndash;59 and/or uACR 3&ndash;30 &rarr; reclassify to a higher category.<br><b>QLD:</b> moderate-to-severe CKD = high risk; non-diabetic sustained eGFR 45&ndash;59 or uACR <b>2.5&ndash;25 (men) / 3.5&ndash;35 (women)</b> &rarr; consider reclassifying when close to threshold. QLD bands are sex-specific; KHA&rsquo;s are not.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.48; QLD Hypertension protocol.</div>"],
["htn", "When does KHA recommend nephrology referral?", "<b>Without delay:</b> rapidly declining eGFR (&gt;15% over 3 months) and/or acute nephritis (oliguria, glomerular haematuria, acute hypertension, oedema).<br><b>Referral recommended:</b> eGFR &lt;30; sustained fall &ge;25% within 12 months or 15 mL/min/1.73m&sup2; per year; persistent uACR &ge;30 mg/mmol; BP not at target despite &ge;3 agents.<br>Note: the p.73 tip words rapid decline as &gt;5 mL/min/1.73m&sup2; over 3 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.73&ndash;74.</div>"],
["htn", "Which commonly used drugs does KHA flag in CKD as nephrotoxic, or as needing dose reduction?", "<b>Avoid (nephrotoxic):</b> lithium, aminoglycosides, NSAIDs/COX-2 inhibitors.<br><b>Reduce or cease (examples):</b> apixaban, dabigatran, rivaroxaban, digoxin, sotalol, spironolactone; gabapentin, pregabalin, opioids; allopurinol, colchicine, baclofen, fenofibrate; valaciclovir, famciclovir, nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim.<br>Dose reduction is generally needed once GFR falls <b>below 60</b>; check product information.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.33, 35.</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["bgl", "Mr T, 58, is asymptomatic. Screening shows venous HbA1c 45 mmol/mol (6.3%) and fasting venous glucose 7.4 mmol/L. What is the correct next step?", ["Diagnose prediabetes, because the HbA1c takes priority when tests disagree", "Repeat the fasting venous glucose on a different day", "Exclude diabetes, as the HbA1c is below 48 mmol/mol", "Diagnose type 2 diabetes now on the fasting glucose alone"], 1, "Discordant tests: repeat the one above the diagnostic threshold (fasting &ge;7.0 mmol/L) and diagnose on the confirmed result. An HbA1c below 48 mmol/mol does not refute a diagnosis made on venous glucose. He is asymptomatic, so one positive result needs confirming on a different day.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "At a pharmacy health check, an asymptomatic 49-year-old has a finger-prick glucose of 12.6 mmol/L after lunch. Which statement is correct?", ["Diabetes is diagnosed, as a random glucose &ge;11.1 mmol/L is diagnostic", "A repeat finger-prick tomorrow at the same time would confirm diabetes", "A capillary result cannot diagnose diabetes; arrange laboratory testing", "A random glucose has no role, so no further testing is needed"], 2, "eTG: do not use point-of-care capillary testing to diagnose diabetes &mdash; use validated laboratory venous samples. The QLD protocol: a screening random glucose &ge;11.1 mmol/L needs a repeat laboratory-confirmed HbA1c or FBG. An asymptomatic positive always needs confirming on a separate day.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026); QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2</div>"],
["bgl", "A 15-year-old has two weeks of thirst, frequent urination and weight loss. A finger-prick glucose is 18 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate action?", ["Arrange a fasting venous glucose and HbA1c, then review next week", "Arrange a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test to confirm the diagnosis", "Advise lifestyle measures and repeat the glucose in one month", "Refer to the emergency department now"], 3, "Hyperglycaemia in a child or adolescent is a medical emergency; presume type 1 diabetes. Waiting for a fasting glucose or HbA1c delays treatment and risks DKA &mdash; point-of-care tests can be used initially. An OGTT has no place in diagnosing type 1. Refer to the emergency department, or phone the nearest children&rsquo;s hospital for endocrinology advice.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "A 34-year-old with polycystic ovary syndrome has a normal fasting venous glucose, but you suspect diabetes. Which test can detect diabetes that fasting glucose may miss?", ["A 2-hour 75 g oral glucose tolerance test", "A fasting insulin concentration", "A repeat fasting glucose on another day", "A C-peptide level taken without a glucose"], 0, "eTG: an OGTT can diagnose type 2 diabetes when fasting glucose alone can underdiagnose because of different patterns of hyperglycaemia &mdash; eg PCOS or glucocorticoid use. It is also the only test that detects impaired glucose tolerance. Insulin concentrations have no role in diagnosis; C-peptide is matched with a glucose, and only when classification is unclear.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "An asymptomatic patient&rsquo;s laboratory HbA1c is 47 mmol/mol. How should this be interpreted?", ["Prediabetes, because 47 mmol/mol sits below the 48 mmol/mol diabetes cut-off", "Diabetes is confirmed on this single result; no further testing is needed", "At the diabetes threshold; confirm on another day", "Normal glucose tolerance; recheck in three years"], 2, "The HbA1c thresholds (42–46 prediabetes, &ge;48 diabetes) do not define 47 mmol/mol. Labs dual-report and generally round 47 up to 6.5% &mdash; the diabetes threshold. Because the patient is asymptomatic, confirm with a repeat test on a different day, preferably the same test.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "Mrs K takes metformin and gliclazide MR. She asks how often to check her blood glucose at home. What does the Diabetes quick guide recommend?", ["Daily: 1–2 checks, fasting plus a random time", "No routine checks; HbA1c alone is enough on tablets", "Four or more checks a day, before each meal and at bedtime", "Only on days when she feels unwell or dizzy"], 0, "Everyone on a sulfonylurea and/or insulin needs daily SMBG. For a sulfonylurea the guide sets 1–2 checks a day, fasting plus random (a different time each day). Four or more is for basal-bolus insulin. She also needs extra checks when unwell, around activity and before driving.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022)</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient with type 2 diabetes on metformin alone has used NDSS-subsidised test strips for six months and is now told she cannot get more. What is needed?", ["A fresh NDSS registration signed by her GP", "A PBS authority prescription for the strips", "Nothing &mdash; strips are not subsidised in type 2 diabetes", "A Blood Glucose Test Strip Six Month Approval form"], 3, "All NDSS registrants can access subsidised strips, but people with type 2 diabetes not using insulin need a Blood Glucose Test Strip Six Month Approval form after the first six months (Diabetes Australia). The quick guide words it as anyone not taking a sulfonylurea or insulin &mdash; a small difference between the two sources.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Australia &mdash; Blood glucose monitoring (web page, Oct 2026); Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022)</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient taking a sulfonylurea asks what his blood glucose must be before he drives.", ["Above 4.0 mmol/L", "Above 5.0 mmol/L", "Above 7.0 mmol/L", "Above 8.0 mmol/L"], 1, "Diabetes Australia and the quick guide: check before driving any vehicle &mdash; you need to be above 5.0 mmol/L. CCM: check BGL is &gt;5 mmol/L before driving and notify the road traffic authority of fitness to drive. 4.0 mmol/L is the hypoglycaemia threshold, not the driving one.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Australia &mdash; Blood glucose monitoring (web page, Oct 2026); Diabetes quick guides: Monitoring (Diabetes Tasmania / Tasmania PHN, v3.0 July 2022); QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient completes the DDS17. Her mean item score is 3.2. How should this be interpreted?", ["Moderate distress worthy of clinical attention &mdash; discuss it", "Minimal distress; act only if a single item scores 6", "Severe distress needing same-day emergency referral", "Not interpretable until the DDS2 is also completed"], 0, "The DDS17 scoring sheet treats a mean item score of 3 or higher as moderate distress worthy of clinical attention, and suggests opening a conversation about any single item scored 3 or more. CCM: regularly assess distress from living with diabetes and refer as necessary.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Distress Screening Scale (DDS2 and DDS17 form with scoring sheet; CCM Resource 5); QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "Mr D, 84, lives alone with heart failure and CKD, and has type 2 diabetes. Using the QLD Chronic Conditions Manual, which targets fit him?", ["Fasting 4–6, 2 h post-meal 4–8 mmol/L; HbA1c &lt;6.5%", "Fasting 4–7, 2 h post-meal 5–10 mmol/L; HbA1c &lt;6.5–7%", "Fasting 6–8, 2 h post-meal 6–12 mmol/L; HbA1c &lt;8.5%", "Fasting 4–8, 2 h post-meal 6–10 mmol/L; HbA1c &lt;7%"], 2, "CCM: type 1 or 2 in the elderly or those living alone with comorbidities &mdash; 6–8 fasting, 6–12 two hours after; HbA1c &lt;8.5%. The QLD protocol words it differently: patients at risk of hypoglycaemia (older people, renal impairment) get a less stringent HbA1c target closer towards 8%. Quote the protocol for Program questions.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024); QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2</div>"],
["bgl", "At a Program review, a patient asks how often she needs an eye examination. What does the Blood Glucose protocol&rsquo;s annual cycle of care (Table 3) specify?", ["At least every 6 months", "At least every 2 years", "At least every 12 months", "Only if her vision changes"], 1, "Protocol Table 3: eye examination at least every 2 years. The QLD Chronic Conditions Manual is stricter &mdash; retinal examination 12-monthly, and refer all newly diagnosed patients to an ophthalmologist or optometrist. Check which source a question is asking about.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD CVD Risk Reduction Program &mdash; Blood Glucose Management Pilot Clinical Protocol V2; QLD Chronic Conditions Manual &mdash; Diabetes (updated Nov 2024)</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient tests twice, from different fingers, and gets 8.1 and 9.3 mmol/L. She thinks her meter is faulty. Best response?", ["Replace the meter now, because any difference above 1 mmol/L means it is faulty", "Average the two readings and record that, since capillary results overestimate", "Stop home testing and rely on HbA1c, as meters are too inaccurate", "Different drops give different results; a gap this size is not usually a concern"], 3, "Diabetes Australia: all meters give a different result with a different drop of blood; unless the difference is more than 2 mmol/L it is not usually a concern. If still worried, check technique (washed, dried hands; strips in date and stored dry) and have the meter checked with control solution.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Diabetes Australia &mdash; Blood glucose monitoring (web page, Oct 2026)</div>"],
["bgl", "You are preparing a patient for a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test. Which instruction is correct?", ["Eat a high-carbohydrate diet for the 3 days beforehand to prepare for it", "Fast overnight for 8–12 hours; water is allowed until the glucose drink", "Sips of water are fine during the 2-hour test, but no food", "A gentle walk during the 2-hour test helps glucose absorption"], 1, "Fast overnight for 8–12 hours, avoiding smoking and caffeine; water is permitted until the glucose is taken. Carbohydrate loading is no longer recommended. During the test the patient stays seated and takes nothing by mouth, including water, for 2 hours. Start before 10 am.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG) &mdash; Classifying and diagnosing diabetes (amended Aug 2022; accessed Oct 2026)</div>"],
["htn", "Ravi, 58, has hypertension. eGFR is 70 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;. Two uACRs two months apart are 5.2 and 4.8 mg/mmol. Under KHA this is CKD stage 2 with A2. What does it mean for the QLD Hypertension Program?", ["Eligible &mdash; the protocol excludes only stages 3&ndash;5 CKD, and he is stage 2", "Ineligible &mdash; persistent uACR &ge;3 mg/mmol; refer to his usual provider", "Eligible, but reclassify his calculated AusCVDRisk to a higher category", "Eligible for now; repeat uACR in 12 months before deciding"], 1, "The protocol excludes stage 3&ndash;5 CKD <b>and</b> nephropathy, defined as persistent albuminuria (uACR &ge;3 mg/mmol) or eGFR &lt;60. Step C lists the same values as a referral trigger. KHA stage 2 A2 is still persistent albuminuria, so the stage 3&ndash;5 wording is the trap. Once an exclusion applies, risk reclassification does not make him eligible.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.16, 21; QLD Hypertension protocol, ineligibility criteria.</div>"],
["htn", "A GP patient with CKD has baseline eGFR 52 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;. Two weeks after starting perindopril, eGFR is 36. Under KHA guidance, what is the right step?", ["Continue &mdash; an early eGFR fall is expected with RAS blockade", "Halve the dose and recheck eGFR and potassium in 3 months", "Cease it and consider nephrology referral", "Add an ARB for extra renal protection and recheck"], 2, "The fall is about 31%. KHA: if the reduction is <b>more than 25% below baseline</b>, cease the ACE inhibitor or ARB and consider referral; under 25% within two weeks, continue. ACE inhibitor + ARB in combination is not recommended. (The QLD protocol uses a different measure &mdash; creatinine rise up to 25%.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.34, 55.</div>"],
["htn", "A patient with CKD on ramipril has a serum potassium of 6.7 mmol/L. What does KHA advise?", ["Low-potassium diet and recheck in one week", "Add a thiazide and continue ramipril", "Cease ramipril and recheck in two weeks", "Refer to the nearest Emergency Department"], 3, "KHA: K+ <b>&gt;6.5 mmol/L</b> &rarr; nearest Emergency Department, because of the risk of arrhythmia. Diet, thiazide, binders and stopping the ACE inhibitor belong to the <b>6.0&ndash;6.5</b> band, and ACEi/ARB/MRA are ceased if K+ stays &gt;6.0 despite those measures.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.65.</div>"],
["bgl", "Helen has CKD and type 2 diabetes, eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;, on ramipril and furosemide. Dapagliflozin is being started. Which KHA advice applies?", ["Consider reducing the furosemide or other antihypertensives", "Order an extra eGFR at one week to catch the expected haemodynamic dip", "Halve the dapagliflozin dose because eGFR is below 45 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;", "Withhold ramipril for the first month while the SGLT2 inhibitor starts"], 0, "SGLT2 inhibitors cause an osmotic diuresis, so KHA suggests considering a reduction in diuretics and/or antihypertensives at initiation. The reversible eGFR drop peaks at 4 weeks and specific testing for it is not required. Dapagliflozin needs no dose adjustment; initiation is not recommended below eGFR 25. ACE inhibitors should continue if tolerated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.34, 50, 55.</div>"],
["htn", "Tom takes ramipril, hydrochlorothiazide, metformin and occasional celecoxib. He has gastroenteritis and cannot keep fluids down. Using SADMANS, what should he withhold?", ["Metformin only &mdash; the others are cardiovascular", "Celecoxib only, as the NSAID causes the harm", "Ramipril only, then restart at half dose", "All four, temporarily, until he is well again"], 3, "SADMANS: sulfonylureas, ACE inhibitors, diuretics, metformin, ARBs, NSAIDs, SGLT2 inhibitors. ACE inhibitor, diuretic and NSAID raise the risk of kidney function decline; metformin raises the risk of adverse events. All are withheld temporarily when fluid intake cannot be maintained and restarted once the condition stabilises. Patients should seek guidance from their health professional.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.33, 35, 41.</div>"],
["htn", "Dana, 50, is not Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander. She smokes and has a BMI of 32, with no diabetes, hypertension or CVD. How often does KHA recommend a Kidney Health Check?", ["Annually, at every health assessment", "Every 2 years", "Once only, then as indicated", "Every 5 years, with her lipid test"], 1, "Obesity and smoking/vaping each call for a Kidney Health Check <b>every 2 years</b>. Annual checks are for diabetes, hypertension and First Nations adults &ge;18. A once-off check applies to people aged &ge;60 with no other indication.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), p.14.</div>"],
["htn", "Which of these people meets a KHA criterion for nephrology referral?", ["Stable eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;, uACR 2.1 mg/mmol", "Single eGFR of 58 on a routine test", "Persistent uACR of 36 mg/mmol", "BP at target on two agents"], 2, "KHA recommends referral for persistent uACR <b>&ge;30 mg/mmol</b>, eGFR &lt;30, a sustained fall of &ge;25% in 12 months or 15 mL/min/1.73m&sup2; per year, or BP not at target on &ge;3 agents. A stable eGFR 48 with normal uACR is managed in primary care; a single eGFR 58 needs repeating, not referral.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.17&ndash;18, 74.</div>"],
["lipid", "Greg, 66, non-Indigenous, has CKD with sustained eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;. He is not on a statin. What does KHA recommend?", ["Start a statin (&plusmn; ezetimibe)", "No statin, because KHA sets no target lipid level in CKD", "A statin only if his LDL-C is above 4.9 mmol/L on a fasting profile", "Avoid statins: eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73m&sup2; increases myopathy risk"], 0, "KHA: eGFR &lt;45 or uACR &gt;30 = pre-determined high risk (&ge;10%), and statin &plusmn; ezetimibe is recommended in CKD with eGFR &ge;15 and risk &ge;10%. Having no lipid target is not a reason to withhold it. LDL-C &gt;4.9 triggers specialist evaluation, not statin start; and KHA recommends statins down to eGFR 15. Stage 3b CKD also puts him outside the QLD Program.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (2024), pp.48, 65.</div>"]
);


/* ---------- U. QLD Smoking Cessation CPG pack + cards (19 Oct 2026) ----------
   Agent-drafted from the QLD CPG (v2.0, Jul 2025) + RACGP algorithm + eTG; all quotes
   machine-checked (drafts/smoking_cpg.json). PACKS.smoke makes smoking appear in Conditions. */
PACKS.smoke = {"prog": "Smoking Cessation (QLD Community Pharmacy CPG)", "out": ["Patient <b>under 18 years</b> &mdash; the service is restricted to patients aged 18 or over. Ineligibility does not preclude usual pharmacy care.", "<b>Vaping cessation</b> &mdash; not within the scope of this guideline (tobacco smoking only), including pharmacotherapy for vaping cessation.", "<b>Nortriptyline</b> and <b>nicotine vaping products</b> are not permitted for use by pharmacists."], "refer": ["<b>Refer</b> (healthcare provider + cessation support service, e.g. Quitline): patient is <b>pregnant</b>.", "<b>Refer</b>: taking a medicine that <b>interacts with smoking</b> and may need review and dose adjustment as smoking patterns change.", "<b>Refer</b>: <b>inadequate response</b> to optimal treatment.", "<b>Treat (if clinically appropriate) and concurrently refer</b>: chronic disease or condition needing extra support or complicating cessation &mdash; e.g. diabetes, CVD, mental illness, substance use disorder, multiple comorbidities.", "<b>Refer</b>: already had 2 courses of varenicline (24 weeks) in the previous 12 months, or needs more varenicline after 2 courses."], "rx": ["A medicine mentioned in <b>Therapeutic Guidelines: Addiction</b> (Tobacco smoking and nicotine dependence), guided by the <b>RACGP pharmacotherapy treatment algorithm</b>.", "Algorithm options: <b>NRT, varenicline, bupropion</b>; combination NRT (patch plus oral) preferred (RACGP).", "Varenicline (CrCl &ge;30 mL/min): 0.5 mg daily &times; 3 days, then 0.5 mg twice daily &times; 4 days, then 1 mg twice daily for the rest of a 12-week course (eTG).", "Bupropion: 150 mg in the morning &times; 3 days, then 150 mg twice a day; 9-week course (eTG).", "Prescribe a quantity (including repeats) aligned with the expected duration of therapy, sufficient until the next review."], "rxnote": "Prescribe according to the specified sections of the <b>current online Therapeutic Guidelines</b>, choosing the medicine with the <b>RACGP pharmacotherapy algorithm</b>. Nortriptyline and nicotine vaping products are <b>not permitted</b>. Varenicline: one extra 12-week course may be considered to reduce relapse; after 2 courses (24 weeks) in 12 months, refer. Supply enough (including repeats) to last until the next review.", "checks": ["<b>Patient history</b>: age; pregnancy and lactation; medical conditions and chronic diseases; current or recently ceased medicines (incl. vitamins, herbs, OTC); allergies/ADRs.", "<b>Psychiatric history</b> (current or past): psychosis, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, depression, anxiety, eating disorder, substance use disorders, suicidal ideation or self-harm.", "<b>Smoking history</b>: age started; type; time to first cigarette; cigarettes per day; cravings/withdrawal; pack years; past quit attempts; past response to pharmacotherapy.", "<b>Dependence</b>: use the eTG screening and assessment section. RACGP: dependent if smoking within 30 minutes of waking, more than 10 cigarettes a day, or withdrawal in previous quit attempts.", "<b>Examination</b>: initial <b>blood pressure</b> to screen for hypertension; screen for CVD, asthma and COPD (Service chronic disease program if eligible, or another provider).", "<b>Confirm</b> via eTG, AMH and product information: contraindications and precautions, <b>drug interactions</b> (smoking itself and cessation medicines), pregnancy and lactation.", "Use the <b>5As</b> to identify support needs, barriers and cultural, population-specific or medicine-associated factors. My Health Record can supply medicines, allergies and conditions."], "nonrx": ["Develop a cessation plan (e.g. <i>Your quit smoking plan</i>, Quit website): barriers, strategies to address them, and social support.", "Not ready to quit: motivational approaches, explore barriers, encourage harm minimisation (fewer cigarettes per day, working towards a quit day).", "Counselling and behavioural support, and <b>referral to Quitline</b> and/or other support programs; QuitHQ has resources for health professionals.", "Coping strategies: delay, deep breathe, drink water, do something else (RACGP)."], "self": ["Quit.org.au: quit plan, quitting tips and tactics, cost of smoking calculator.", "Queensland Government QuitHQ: smoking cost calculator and <b>My Quit Journey</b> 12-week program.", "Commonwealth Government <b>My QuitBuddy</b> app."], "counsel": ["Instructions for the dosage form and medicine use, e.g. dose, NRT product selection and use; written information if required.", "How to manage adverse effects, including <b>serious</b> ones needing medical care, e.g. <b>mood disturbances</b>.", "When to return for follow-up.", "Relapse is not uncommon: do not be discouraged &mdash; most former smokers went through several attempts and relapses before long-term cessation.", "Varenicline: up to 30% get nausea, reduced by taking with food (eTG). Bupropion: insomnia is the most common adverse effect (eTG)."], "safety": ["Seek support from a healthcare provider and cessation service (e.g. Quitline) after a <b>relapse</b>, whenever extra help is needed, or with any concerns.", "Serious adverse effects such as <b>mood disturbances</b> need medical care.", "Varenicline: if nicotine withdrawal symptoms occur in the first week, stop varenicline and seek medical advice (eTG)."], "review": "NRT: review at 2 weeks (tolerability, effectiveness), then at regular intervals during the minimum 12-week course. Varenicline or bupropion: review at 2 weeks (progress, adverse effects, <b>blood pressure</b>, relapse-prevention support), and again at end of course (9 weeks bupropion, 12 weeks varenicline). Ongoing support from Quitline is also recommended. After that, individualise follow-up. Share the plan with the care team, with consent."};
LINKS.push({"id": "smoke", "name": "SMOKING CESSATION — tools to hand the patient", "items": [{"n": "Quit — Your quit smoking plan (build a personal quit plan)", "u": "https://www.quit.org.au/en/tools/quit-plan/smoking/1", "w": "The QLD CPG suggests a written quit plan: barriers, strategies, support. Walk the patient through it or send the link."}, {"n": "Quit HQ (Queensland Health) — for health professionals", "u": "https://www.quithq.initiatives.qld.gov.au/for-health-professionals", "w": "QLD referral pathways, My Quit Journey 12-week program, cost calculator. Named in the QLD CPG."}, {"n": "QLD Smoking Cessation clinical practice guideline", "u": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0023/1304249/smoking-cessation-guideline.pdf", "w": "The Program document: 18+, refer/treat-and-refer lists, eTG + RACGP algorithm, 2-course varenicline cap.", "t": "Governs practice"}]});
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: what is the minimum age for the prescribing service, and what can an ineligible patient still receive?", "Restricted to patients aged <b>18 years or over</b>. Being ineligible for the prescribing service does <b>not</b> preclude access to <b>usual pharmacy care</b>.<br><b>Contrast</b>: the RACGP algorithm calls NRT suitable for many smokers, <i>including adolescents</i>, but that does not extend the QLD service below 18.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP pharmacotherapy algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: in which three situations do you <b>refer</b> rather than treat?", "Refer to an appropriate healthcare provider <b>and</b> a cessation support service (e.g. Quitline) when:<br>&bull; the patient is <b>pregnant</b><br>&bull; they take a medicine that <b>interacts with smoking</b> and may need review and dose adjustment as smoking patterns change<br>&bull; there is an <b>inadequate response</b> to optimal treatment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: when do you <b>treat and concurrently refer</b>?", "When the patient has a <b>chronic disease or medical condition</b> that needs extra support or can complicate cessation, e.g. <b>diabetes, cardiovascular disease, mental illness, substance use disorder, multiple comorbidities</b>.<br>Give initial treatment (if clinically appropriate) and concurrently refer to a healthcare provider and a support service (e.g. Quitline).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "Does the QLD Smoking Cessation CPG cover vaping, and which products may a pharmacist <b>not</b> use?", "It covers <b>tobacco smoking</b> only. Vaping cessation, including pharmacotherapy for it, is outside its scope.<br><b>Nortriptyline</b> and <b>nicotine vaping products</b> are not permitted for use by pharmacists.<br><b>Contrast</b>: RACGP Recommendation 14 lists nortriptyline as a second-line agent, but it is not available to the QLD pharmacist prescriber.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation, Rec 14</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: what examination is required at the first visit, and which comorbidities should be screened for?", "Take an initial <b>blood pressure</b> to screen for hypertension.<br>Screen for common smoking-related comorbidities (<b>CVD, asthma and COPD</b>), either in a Service chronic disease program (if eligible) or with an appropriate healthcare provider.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "Which <b>smoking history</b> items does the QLD Smoking Cessation CPG list?", "&bull; Age smoking started; type (e.g. cigarettes, pipes)<br>&bull; Time to first cigarette in the morning; cigarettes per day<br>&bull; Cravings, urges or withdrawal on stopping<br>&bull; Duration: past and current <b>pack years</b><br>&bull; Previous quit attempts and smoke-free periods, and support needed<br>&bull; Previous responses to cessation pharmacotherapy<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "Which <b>psychiatric history</b> does the QLD Smoking Cessation CPG ask you to record, and why does it matter for bupropion?", "Current or past psychiatric illness, including psychosis, schizophrenia, <b>bipolar disorder</b>, clinically diagnosed depression and anxiety, <b>eating disorder</b>, substance use disorders, and suicidal ideation, suicide-related behaviour or self-harm.<br>eTG: bupropion is <b>contraindicated</b> with eating disorders and needs <b>caution</b> in bipolar disorder (risk of mania).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); eTG Bupropion</div>"],
["smoke", "What may a QLD pharmacist prescribe for smoking cessation, and what guides the choice?", "A medicine mentioned in <b>Therapeutic Guidelines: Addiction</b> (Tobacco smoking and nicotine dependence), guided by the <b>RACGP pharmacotherapy treatment algorithm</b>. That algorithm offers NRT, varenicline and bupropion. Prescribing must follow the specified sections of the <b>current online</b> eTG.<br>Not permitted: nortriptyline, nicotine vaping products.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP pharmacotherapy algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: when should a patient prescribed <b>NRT</b> be followed up?", "<b>2 weeks</b> after starting, to review tolerability and effectiveness, then at regular intervals during the <b>minimum 12-week course</b>. Ongoing support from a service such as Quitline is also recommended.<br><b>Contrast</b>: the RACGP algorithm says to encourage completion of <i>at least 10 weeks</i> of NRT.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP pharmacotherapy algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: what is the review schedule for <b>varenicline or bupropion</b>, and what is done at 2 weeks?", "<b>At 2 weeks</b>: check progress and adverse effects, <b>measure blood pressure</b>, and give extra support (especially relapse prevention).<br><b>At end of course</b>: <b>9 weeks</b> for bupropion, <b>12 weeks</b> for varenicline.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: when can varenicline be extended, and when must you refer? How does eTG differ?", "An <b>extra 12 weeks</b> may be considered if needed to reduce the chance of relapse.<br><b>Refer</b> if the patient has already had <b>2 courses (24 weeks) in the previous 12 months</b>, or needs more varenicline after 2 courses.<br><b>Contrast</b>: eTG says to offer a repeat 12-week course to anyone who wishes to continue, with no cap. The 2-course limit comes from the QLD Program.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); eTG Varenicline</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: how much medicine should you prescribe? How does this compare with the RACGP algorithm?", "QLD: prescribe a quantity (<b>including repeats</b>) that matches the expected duration of therapy, with enough supply to last until the <b>next review</b>.<br>RACGP algorithm: an initial <b>four-week</b> varenicline script or an initial <b>two-week</b> bupropion script, with a return visit for the second script.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP pharmacotherapy algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: what should determine the type of help offered, and how do you support someone not ready to quit?", "Base the type of assistance on <b>willingness to quit</b>, needs, preferences and <b>level of nicotine dependence</b>.<br>If not ready: use motivational approaches, explore barriers, and encourage <b>harm minimisation</b>, such as reducing cigarettes smoked each day and working towards a quit day.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: what counselling should go with the management plan, and who receives the plan?", "&bull; How to use the dosage form and medicine (dose, NRT product selection and use)<br>&bull; How to manage adverse effects, including <b>serious</b> ones needing medical care (e.g. <b>mood disturbances</b>)<br>&bull; When to return for follow-up<br>Give written information if required. Share the plan with the multidisciplinary team, <b>with the patient&rsquo;s consent</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG: what should patients be told about relapse?", "Even with effective pharmacotherapy, relapse is <b>not uncommon</b>. Patients should not be discouraged by unsuccessful attempts: most people who have quit went through several attempts and relapses before long-term cessation.<br>If they relapse, need extra help or have concerns, they should seek support from a healthcare provider and a cessation service (e.g. Quitline).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG, <i>Confirm management is appropriate</i>: what must you check, and in which references?", "Use eTG, AMH and other resources (including product information) to check:<br>&bull; contraindications and precautions<br>&bull; <b>drug interactions</b><br>&bull; pregnancy and lactation<br>Both <b>smoking itself</b> and cessation pharmacotherapy cause drug interactions that need considering when someone reduces or stops smoking.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["smoke", "A 17-year-old who smokes 15 cigarettes a day asks a Queensland pharmacist prescriber for varenicline under the Smoking Cessation CPG. What applies?", ["Prescribe varenicline with a 2-week review, since he is clearly motivated to quit", "Ineligible for the prescribing service; usual pharmacy care is still available", "Prescribe NRT only, because the RACGP algorithm says it suits adolescents", "No pharmacy help of any kind; refer to Quitline only"], 1, "The QLD service is restricted to patients aged <b>18 or over</b>, but ineligibility <b>does not preclude usual pharmacy care</b>, so D is wrong. The RACGP algorithm calls NRT suitable <i>including adolescents</i>, but that does not extend the QLD prescribing service.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP pharmacotherapy algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "A 45-year-old with type 2 diabetes smokes 20 a day and wants to quit. She is not pregnant and takes no medicine that interacts with smoking. Under the QLD CPG, what should the pharmacist do?", ["Refer only, as a chronic disease excludes her from the prescribing service entirely", "Treat without referral, because her diabetes is stable and she is otherwise well", "Treat if clinically appropriate, and concurrently refer to a provider and Quitline", "Defer treatment until her GP has reviewed her diabetes"], 2, "A chronic disease that needs extra support or can complicate cessation (diabetes is a named example) means <b>treat (if clinically appropriate) and concurrently refer</b> to a healthcare provider and a cessation service such as Quitline. It is not an exclusion, and referral is still required.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "A woman 26 weeks pregnant asks a Queensland pharmacist prescriber for nicotine patches under the Smoking Cessation CPG. What is correct?", ["Prescribe a 16-hour nicotine patch, as eTG advises using the 16-hour patch in pregnancy", "Prescribe oral NRT only, then review in 2 weeks", "Prescribe varenicline, as the most effective single option", "Refer to a healthcare provider and a cessation service (e.g. Quitline)"], 3, "Under the QLD CPG, <b>pregnancy means refer</b>, not treat. eTG does say to use a 16-hour patch in pregnancy, and RACGP says NRT is a possible option in pregnancy under medical supervision. Those pathways belong to the supervising clinician, not the Program. Varenicline is not a first-line choice in pregnancy (eTG).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); eTG NRT; RACGP algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "A patient wants to quit smoking. Her psychiatrist has noted that one of her psychotropic medicines is metabolised by CYP1A2. Under the QLD CPG, what should the pharmacist do?", ["Refer to a healthcare provider and Quitline", "Start combination NRT, because nicotine itself does not induce CYP1A2", "Start varenicline and refer at the same time", "Start bupropion, which avoids the interaction"], 0, "QLD CPG: a medicine that <b>interacts with smoking</b> and may need review and dose adjustment as smoking changes means <b>refer</b>, not treat-and-refer. RACGP: the tar in tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2, so when smoking is reduced or stopped, CYP1A2 drugs may need a lower dose and closer monitoring. That holds whichever cessation medicine is used.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation</div>"],
["smoke", "A patient has completed two 12-week varenicline courses in the past 10 months, relapsed, and asks for a third. Under the QLD CPG, what applies?", ["Prescribe a third course, since eTG offers repeats to anyone", "Switch to nortriptyline as a second-line agent", "Refer to an appropriate healthcare provider", "Prescribe a third course with a BP check at 2 weeks"], 2, "QLD: anyone who has already had <b>2 courses (24 weeks) within 12 months</b>, or needs more after 2 courses, should be <b>referred</b>. eTG offers repeat courses to anyone who wishes to continue, but the QLD Program sets the cap. Nortriptyline is not permitted for pharmacists.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); eTG Varenicline</div>"],
["smoke", "A pharmacist prescribes bupropion under the QLD CPG. When are follow-up consultations scheduled?", ["At 2 weeks only, as all later follow-up is handed over to the patient&rsquo;s GP", "At 2 weeks (including blood pressure), then at 9 weeks at the end of the course", "At 2 weeks (including blood pressure), then at 12 weeks at the end of the course", "At 1 week, then at 7 weeks"], 1, "Varenicline or bupropion: review at <b>2 weeks</b> (progress, adverse effects, <b>blood pressure</b>, relapse-prevention support), then at the <b>end of the course</b>: <b>9 weeks for bupropion</b>, 12 weeks for varenicline. The RACGP algorithm encourages at least 7 weeks of bupropion, but the course length is 9 weeks.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025); RACGP algorithm</div>"],
["smoke", "An adult who uses only nicotine vapes, and has never smoked tobacco, asks for prescribed help to quit under the QLD Smoking Cessation CPG. What applies?", ["Prescribe varenicline, as for tobacco smoking", "Outside this guideline&rsquo;s scope, which covers tobacco smoking only", "Prescribe nortriptyline as a non-nicotine option", "Supply a reducing-strength nicotine vape under the CPG, with a 2-week review"], 1, "The CPG covers <b>tobacco smoking</b> cessation. Vaping cessation, including pharmacotherapy for it, is <b>not within its scope</b>. Under the guideline, pharmacists may not use nortriptyline or nicotine vaping products.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"],
["smoke", "At the first QLD smoking-cessation consultation, which examination does the CPG specify?", ["An initial blood pressure measurement to screen for hypertension", "Spirometry for every patient before any pharmacotherapy is started", "An exhaled carbon monoxide reading to confirm current smoking status", "A baseline HbA1c before starting varenicline"], 0, "The CPG says to <b>take an initial blood pressure</b> to screen for hypertension. It recommends screening for CVD, asthma and COPD, either in a Service chronic disease program (if eligible) or with an appropriate provider, but it does not require spirometry, CO monitoring or HbA1c at this visit.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Community Pharmacy Smoking Cessation CPG (v2.0, 1 July 2025)</div>"]
);
